// The boss journal: Vhalgrath, the Ember Wyrm, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
// Revamp pages: Cinder Feast (now a party-wide blow on the knight, in bites) and Twin Breath (numbered carriers) rewritten; Skyward and The Tyrant Wakes
// (the Act II and Act IV openers) added, plus Take Flight (a light gust, always uncovered); Marked Prey, Ember Devils and Molten Trail added, Extreme only (extreme: true).
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).wyrm = {
  // the first time this hero meets him (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "Past the road the world is bigger than any wall I have stood behind. The dragon follows whoever holds him, then leaves the ground altogether and comes down where he pleases. A line cannot hold what is not there. I learned to watch the sky, and kept my feet on what was left.",
    gloomsworn: "Out past the road there are skies without ceilings. The Wyrm follows whoever stands before him, then leaves the earth and comes down wherever he pleases. I cannot guard what is not there. I looked up. I have lain under worse skies, and I was dead for those.",
    thresher: "Beyond the road everything is larger, the dragon included, and what a view. Vhalgrath is weather with wings: drought on the ground, then hail from a clear sky wherever he pleases. I watched where the embers drew his road down, and kept to cooler ground, hat in hand.",
    bloodreaver: "Past the road, and finally a real fire in him! Claws like plough blades, a breath that cooks the floor, and then he flew off before I'd finished enjoying it. Cheeky. I want him back on the ground. I have never been so sure I'm in the right place.",
    runeguard: "Beyond the road the old texts run out, and the Wyrm writes his own. Two courts: the air gives plenty of warning; the ground gives none that I have yet read. I began a new volume and left the ground chapter blank. Not foreseen; now it will be.",
    gorgemaw: "Beyond the road: a roast of the first rank, a great black beast with fire in the belly, served from the sky. I am told it is too hot to eat. I shall be the judge of that. I came away hungry, and singed, and happier than I have been in weeks.",
    oathbroker: "The Tyrant holds the crater by right of age and enforces it by fire, in terms read out in the air before they land. One must be there to read them. I was not; the lapse is mine. Beyond the road the contracts are older and the penalties better funded.",
    ironpalm: "He came down from the sky and spoke with fire. I answered too slowly. The ground here is warm and uneven, and the road is far behind us. Listen to the wings before the claws. I will remember. Breathe.",
    ranger: "Big. Black. Flies when it chooses, lands where it likes. No line on a thing that won't stay on the ground. Watched where it came from, not where it went. New country, bigger quarry. I'm not complaining. Much.",
    shadowblade: "The mark has the whole sky to hide in, which is cheating. When it comes down it comes down loudly. I waited at its flank and counted its blind side. First time I have been out-shadowed by the weather. Will try again.",
    pyromancer: "A DRAGON! Fire, and he is better at it than I am! I have never been so insulted, or so delighted. The floor glowed the colour of trouble all night and I followed it like a moth. Beyond the road is wonderful.",
    thermist: "Hot. All of him, and the ground he spat on. I glowed with admiration and burnt my boots. The sky was cold; that part I liked. Beyond the road the readings go off the page. I shall need a bigger notebook.",
    driftblade: "He took off and I wanted to follow him, which is not allowed, and then he came down like a falling mountain on the far side of the crater, and I swept through the ash for the whole of it. The road ends here and the wind does not.",
    marionettist: "Our leading man arrives by air, which is vulgar and effective. Three scorched marks in the floor, and he picks one for his entrance. The company, a long way from the road, was not cued. (My Doll was singed, and sulked.)",
    cleric: "An old tyrant on a hot hill, and nobody he did not burn a little. He is not cruel; he is simply very large. I had more to mend than I could reach, far from any road home. Dawn keep the weary. Back to it.",
    grovekeeper: "A drought and a fire in one animal. The ground does not take the seed where he has breathed. Everything he does comes from above, like weather, and I am not used to weather that chooses. We are far from the road, and the weeds are larger here.",
    chronomancer: "He rises, he falls, he lands, and each time he lands I am standing in the wrong place. The warning is long, the beat is exact, and I found it only on the fourth try. Larger country, same lesson. Not my first time.",
    alembist: "Specimen: one dragon, very old, very warm. Symptoms: burns, shocks, and a floor that bites for some time after. Shelves ran low by the first descent. One flask went off. Not mine! Beyond the road, other people's flasks go off. Noted.",
    threadweaver: "He hovers over the whole crater like a loose thread and drops where he pleases. My stitches kept running into the last place he had been. A sloppy seam, entirely mine, and a long way from home with no spare thread.",
    tidecaller: "Past the road the sky is the larger water, and I had not learned its tides. The Wyrm walked after the knight, then rose and came down wherever he pleased, and I mended half a swell behind him all night. Tomorrow I plan the water for his flights.",
    lanternkeeper: "Past the road the sky is the larger dark, and I had never lit it. The Wyrm came down wherever he pleased, and my lanterns were never where we needed them. Tomorrow I light where he will land, not where he has been. Keep your light, even up here.",
    kenseier: "A dragon has no rhythm worth the name: he walks, he rises, he comes down where he pleases. I spent the night learning the one beat he kept, his claws on me, and watching the sky for the rest. Tomorrow I count the Wyrm. Ash settles.",
    houndmaster: "Black dragon, flies when he likes and lands where he pleases. No tracking a thing that won't stay put. Both dogs flat on their bellies the whole night. Tomorrow I watch where he comes from, not where he went.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats him
  win: {
    bastion: "The Wyrm is down. The line held through the flights and the burning rim, and the crater is quiet. A long way past the Barrow, and still standing. I counted heads on a warm rock and said nothing for a while.",
    gloomsworn: "He is down. The crater is quiet, and I am still here, which still surprises me. Nothing in the roost or on the rim is watching me now. Somebody else may keep the watch a while.",
    thresher: "Vhalgrath has come in at last, and the crater may go quiet. I said a few kind words over him; he was old weather, and old weather has earned them. Something new stirs in me, not yet due.",
    bloodreaver: "The big lizard is down, and every scar he gave me is still warm. The best fire I ever took, and I took all of it. I'll remember his heart opening for the rest of my life. What's next?",
    runeguard: "The Wyrm is judged, and the air with him. I read the sky at last: every line drawn before it was walked, every fire shown before it fell. The blank chapter is filled.",
    gorgemaw: "The roast is carved. Smoke, ash and a touch of old bone. I ate until I could not feel my teeth, and it was worth every burn. Slightly overdone. Magnificent. Beyond the road, they know how to cook.",
    oathbroker: "The Tyrant's claim on the crater is voided, and the account is closed. We paid in full, and the final instalment came due in the heart of him. Countersigned. A larger ledger now, and a lighter pen.",
    ironpalm: "He spoke his last word with fire, and we answered it from the ground. The crater is quiet now. The road is long behind us and longer ahead.",
    ranger: "The old dragon is down. The wind in the crater is mine now. Clean line, at last. A whole sky to myself and nothing in it.",
    shadowblade: "The mark came down and stayed down. For once the sky was empty, and so was he. It never saw me, and in the end it never saw anything. Delicious.",
    pyromancer: "I outshone a DRAGON! Briefly, and only in the last moment, but it counts. My robes are a disgrace. My heart is not. Mostly unsinged, if you do not look at the sleeves.",
    thermist: "He fell, and the whole crater went still. Hot to the end, then cold, and a quiet in the middle where I like to stand. At last. Readings: all of them. I shall need a week to file.",
    driftblade: "He fell out of the sky for the last time, and I flew out through the smoke and up the ribs of older kills and never once landed, and the wind went with me, all the way to the horizon.",
    marionettist: "The star of the air has had his last scene. The company stood to the very end, and the Doll took the last bow. (Applause. The crater echoed it. A fine house, so far from home.)",
    cleric: "The old tyrant is at rest, and the crater is cooling. We walked out from under his wings, all of us, scorched boots and all. Thank the light, and thank every one of us who stood in it. The road home is long, but it is ours.",
    grovekeeper: "The fire is out of him, dear. The crater will take seed again, in time. Leave the ashes alone for a season. My lot came through all of it. Plant first, and let the ground do its own thinking.",
    chronomancer: "He fell, and I did not have to undo a thing. That is the first time with a dragon. I shall be proud of us, I suppose. I am now. And a little afraid of how large the next thing is.",
    alembist: "Result: one dragon, extinguished. Shelves nearly bare, every patient alive. A new reagent has appeared in the heart of the crater, not mine. Labelled: do not touch. Touched anyway. Mildly warm.",
    threadweaver: "He is unpicked at last. There is a seam in the sky where he used to hang, and I did not tie it. Everyone came home. Pulled it tight regardless. Needle up, and breathing again.",
    tidecaller: "Vhalgrath is down, and the crater lies quiet as a dry harbour. The water was high every time he fell on us, and it held. A long way past the road, and the sea gives back.",
    lanternkeeper: "Vhalgrath is down, and the crater is cooling. Four of us climbed out of it, and the lanterns I set were still burning when we left. A long way from any street. I'll leave one burning on the rim.",
    kenseier: "The Wyrm is down and the crater is quiet. He was the first enemy whose rests I had to learn as well as his strokes. I sat on a warm stone and let the ash fall.",
    houndmaster: "Dragon's down and the crater's gone quiet. Ash found a warm stone and Rust found the bones. Smoky supper, and a late one.",
  },
  moves: {
    "Tyrant's Roar": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He roared, and it reached every one of us. Nowhere to stand that was not in it. I kept the line close to our healer, boots set, and took it standing. A shield means nothing against a sound. I held it up out of respect for the shield.",
      gloomsworn: "His roar went through every one of us, and no shield was any use against sound. I stood close to the one who mends and let the dark take its share of me. It was a long note. I have heard lower, from the grave.",
      thresher: "His roar falls on all of us at once, and there is nowhere to hide. I had myself topped up before his throat swelled and kept my hands busy through it. Quite a voice, though. Few carry like that.",
      bloodreaver: "The roar went through my ribs and every one of my friends' too! Nothing in it for me, shameful, but they felt it, so I stayed by the healer and let them be topped up after. Ha. He's got lungs.",
      runeguard: "His throat swells and the air shakes. The roar reaches the whole crater and no ward turns it, so each of us must be whole before it sounds. I annotated it in a steady hand. The page shook anyway.",
      gorgemaw: "The roar is a course served to every seat at once, and I cannot eat it for anyone. So we sat tight, came to it well fed, and shared the swallow. Smoky, with a long finish, like an old cheese.",
      oathbroker: "The Roar is a levy on every party, payable at once; no clause excuses anyone. One must arrive at it already mended, and we had. He offered no terms. I entered an objection regardless.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke to all of us at once, loudly. There is no answering it. We stood still and let it run through us. After, the crater hummed, and I hummed with it a little, to be polite.",
      ranger: "Roar. Reaches everyone, nowhere to stand out of it. Mended before, drew right through it. Ears rang all night.",
      shadowblade: "The mark roared and it found everyone, even me, which was rude. Nothing to hide behind, so I made sure I was whole when it sounded and went back to work. Loud people are such a bore.",
      pyromancer: "The roar shook the cave, and he does it better than I do! No escaping it, so I made sure I was mended before it came and kept casting through it. A fine voice. Needs coaching.",
      thermist: "The roar goes through everyone. Fact. Be mended ahead of it. Then it hit and I hummed with it, rattled, still swinging, every tooth humming. Frequency: low. Volume: yes. Result: unrepeatable.",
      driftblade: "He roared across the whole crater and there was no slipping through it or round it, nowhere to go, which offends me, so I came to it whole and kept moving through the sound like a swimmer through surf.",
      marionettist: "The star opened his mouth and the whole house shook, stalls and wings alike. No exits; the cast came to it whole and played on. (The Doll stood unmoved. I admired her. I flinched.)",
      cleric: "The roar fell on every one of us. I mended everyone before it came, not after, and laid a little grace over my friends as he lifted his head. Even a dragon, I think, is lonely up there.",
      grovekeeper: "The roar comes like hail: no cover, my lot all at once. The mending was growing in everyone before it broke. A dragon can shake the whole valley and not shake a well-planted root.",
      chronomancer: "The roar lands just after his throat swells. I had everyone mended in the moment before; next time, which was last time, I will start earlier. I rather enjoyed the echo. It went on a long while.",
      alembist: "The roar is felt by everyone, with no hiding from it. Best treated beforehand: the whole party whole before it sounds, the big brew poured just ahead. Front shelf, and tape down the corks; things rattle in a crater.",
      threadweaver: "The roar reached every stitch together. No tying it off. We stood close, I pulled the slack tight, and we took it in common. Mind the slack; it is the first thing a dragon finds.",
      tidecaller: "The roar rolled over every one of us, and there is no hiding from sound. We stood close, and I let the water climb as he lifted his head, so it was already high when the roar broke. A gull rides out the loudest gale.",
      lanternkeeper: "The roar rolled over every one of us, and there is no hiding from sound. We stood close inside my glows, and I let every lantern burst as he lifted his head, so the roar fell on a party already mended.",
      kenseier: "He roared, and it reached every one of us. A blade cannot turn a sound, so we gathered by the healer and took it standing. Mended first, then still. That answers all such blows.",
      houndmaster: "He roared, and it reached every one of us. Nowhere to hide from a noise. Stood by the healer, dogs against my legs, mended first, then took it upright.",
    } },
    'Rending Claws': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The claws came down on me alone, a wide rake in front of him. I waited for the shoulder to drop, shield up in the last instant, and kept the others out of the arc. Claws like that make the Barrow King seem a gentleman.",
      gloomsworn: "The claws were meant for me. I watched his near shoulder and let the rake land where I stood. It found nothing in me that was not already scarred. The rest kept wide of the arc.",
      thresher: "The claws come down on our knight, a wide rake across the front of him, a curtain drawn the wrong way. I kept to his flank, well out of the arc, and went on working his side. Not my appointment, that one, and our knight keeps it well.",
      bloodreaver: "The claws! A deep one across the front of him, and the wound stays good and open. I braced, took it, and answered it while it was fresh. Again, my friend, please.",
      runeguard: "He rears and the near claw lifts. A ward a breath before; the others well outside the arc in front of him. I had written the lift of the claw in the margin before it came. I count that as the first achievement of the new volume.",
      gorgemaw: "The claws are served to me alone, a heavy rake with a sauce that runs. I opened wide just before it, kept my friends off my plate, and chewed. Rare, and good. Next time, a little less char.",
      oathbroker: "The Claws are payable by whoever stands before him, and the arc runs wide. I accepted delivery behind every protection I had, the rest of us kept clear. A dragon's signature is a claw. I did not dispute it.",
      ironpalm: "He raked once, wide. I did not raise my arms until its last instant, and took half. Then I answered. Not every blow deserves a long reply.",
      ranger: "Claws. Wide arc in front of it. Kept to its flank, outside the rake, and drew again before the dust settled. Fast animal. Slow lesson.",
      shadowblade: "The mark raked at whoever faced it, which was not me. I kept to its side, where neither claw could find me, and came back in when it was done.",
      pyromancer: "He raked the ground in front of him and I decided I did not need to be there! I stood off to the side and finished my spell from the edge. A good claw. Very theatrical. Not for me.",
      thermist: "Claws: wide, in front. Step to the side. Then the heat came and I swung back in at once, cutting, behind the swing and not in it. Result: unburned. Conclusion: stand wherever the claw isn't.",
      driftblade: "The rake came round in front of him and I slipped along his side, out the back of it and round again without touching down, and it was over before I had even slowed, like a gust through a grove.",
      marionettist: "The leading man swept the stage in front of him. The cast stepped to the wings on cue and my Doll slipped out behind. (Applause for the Doll, mostly.)",
      cleric: "The claws fell on our knight, as they always do. I laid the light on him before they landed instead of after, and he barely staggered. Old habit, young bones.",
      grovekeeper: "The big rake came on like a squall, front of him only. I had planted on our knight before it, so the mending was already growing when it struck. Let the storm be late. I will be early.",
      chronomancer: "The claws come down on the beat after the shoulder drops. I warded our knight in the gap before it, and was early. Not for the first time, nor the last, nor, possibly, the current.",
      alembist: "The claws, one heavy rake, front of him only: a blue ward on whoever stands there, and everyone else kept out of the arc. Label it plainly. A heavy rake needs a heavy bottle.",
      threadweaver: "The rake landed on our knight, and it ran down my thread to the archer, so neither took all of it. Good needlework. Keep to the sides, you lot, and leave the front to him.",
      tidecaller: "The claws were meant for the knight alone, a wide rake in front of him, so the rest of us kept to his sides. I watched his shoulder and had the water high around the knight before it fell, not after.",
      lanternkeeper: "The claws were for our knight alone, a wide rake in front of him, so the rest of us kept to his sides. I had a lantern hung on the knight before the blow and kept its wick gold.",
      kenseier: "The claws came down on me alone, a wide rake before him. I met them with the blade as they landed, and the others stayed out of the arc. Each rake had the same count, and by the end I answered it without thinking.",
      houndmaster: "Claws came down on the shield-man, a wide rake in front of him. Kept to his flank, outside the rake. Dogs too. Don't send a hound at his face.",
    } },
    'Ember Breath': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He breathed at one of us, not me, and the fan of fire followed them. I kept the line clear of the long flames and off the glowing ground it left behind. Fire does not respect a post. I do.",
      gloomsworn: "The breath chose someone else, and followed them. I kept clear of its length and off the scorched floor after, which lingers. I know something of lingering.",
      thresher: "The breath chooses one of us and follows them to the last. Whoever it is waits, then steps aside late, not back. The ground it scorches stays foul a good while; I kept off it as I would a bonfire still smoking.",
      bloodreaver: "A cone of fire! And it follows whoever he picks until the last moment. I like that. Step aside late and sideways, and keep off the molten ground after. A fine scar, not mine.",
      runeguard: "His head goes back and the fire gathers; it tracks its quarry until the last. Leave the fan sideways, late, and stay off the molten ground it leaves. I noted that the breath obeys the rule of a good argument: it follows until the last possible moment.",
      gorgemaw: "The breath is a flambé, served at one guest and following them round the table. Step out of the line at the last, and do not stand on the plate afterwards. Overcooked, but fashionably so.",
      oathbroker: "The Breath is served on one named party, follows him, and then lingers on the floor. Clear the line, then clear the ground. Two clauses, one injury.",
      ironpalm: "Fire came for one of us. It followed. I did not run; I stepped aside, late. Then I stayed off the hot ground.",
      ranger: "Breath at one of us. Tracks till late. Stepped out sideways at the last, not back. The floor burns a while after. Don't stand on it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark breathed at someone else, which was lovely of it. The fire follows them until the last moment, so they step aside late. The ground burns afterwards. I stayed well clear of the floor.",
      pyromancer: "He breathed fire at one of us and I felt I should have been consulted! It follows them until the last instant; step off sideways and keep clear of the glowing floor. Professional jealousy aside, a very fine cone.",
      thermist: "Breath: hot, narrow, tracking. Step aside, late. Then the ground stays molten, stay off it! I wasn't cold enough once and stepped on it. Result: boots reduced to a theory. Bootless, but resolved.",
      driftblade: "The fire came after one of us in a long sweep and I cut through its edge and out the side just as it set, and the floor behind it was glowing and I went round that too.",
      marionettist: "The star breathed at one member of the cast, and the beam followed them across the stage. Sidestep at the last cue; the boards afterward burn for a time. (The Doll walked round them.)",
      cleric: "The fire hunted one of my friends and followed her across the floor. Wait, I told her, and step aside late, and mind the floor after. Child, you are faster than you think. She was.",
      grovekeeper: "The breath comes like a grass fire, dear, in a line after one of my lot. Wait, step aside, and keep off the scorched ground; nothing grows there for a time. Plant after, never on.",
      chronomancer: "The fire tracks its quarry until the last breath. I stepped aside on that note and not before. The ground burned for a time after. Next time, which was last time, I stood further off.",
      alembist: "A cone of fire at one of us, tracking, and a floor that burns afterwards: step aside late, then keep off the glow. Label the floor too: this side hot.",
      threadweaver: "The fire chased one stitch across the floor. Wait for the last moment, step out sideways, and keep every thread off the glowing ground. A burnt hem is a sloppy hem.",
      tidecaller: "The fire hunted one of us and followed whoever it chose across the floor. They waited for the last moment and stepped out sideways; the rest kept clear of its length, and all of us kept off the glowing ground it left. Scorched floor stays hot a long while.",
      lanternkeeper: "The fire hunted one of us and followed them across the floor. They waited until the last moment and stepped out sideways. The rest kept clear of its length, and I set my lanterns well away from the scorch it left.",
      kenseier: "He breathed at one of the others and the fan of fire followed them. We kept clear of the long flames and off the glowing ground it left. It is a long stroke, not a quick one. Plenty of time to leave.",
      houndmaster: "He breathed on one of us, flame following them nearly to the last. Sidestepped late, never backed away. The scorched floor lingers, so keep the hounds off it.",
    } },
    'Tail Lash': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He swept the tail round behind him, not in front. I held my place at his front and the line stood to his sides. Whoever stood at his back learned it quickly. A tail is a rear guard of its own.",
      gloomsworn: "The tail swung behind him. I stood before his face, the rest at his flanks. Whoever stood at his back learned it the way I learned dying: once.",
      thresher: "The tail sweeps round behind him in a long, slow arc. I work from his side, never his back, and it passes me by with a swish. Whoever stood behind him learned the lesson once, and it is an easy one.",
      bloodreaver: "The tail! Behind him, a wide sweep, and I was in front for once. A good one, but not mine. The ones behind him were not so pleased. Stay at his sides.",
      runeguard: "His shoulders turn and the tail rises. The sweep is behind him, so the line stands at his sides or his front, and no one behind. Rear guard is a poor posting. I have never advised anyone to take it.",
      gorgemaw: "The tail is a side dish, served at the back of the table. Whoever sat at the back got the lot. I sat at the head, as a gourmand should.",
      oathbroker: "The location of the Tail is the only term that matters: behind him, never in front. Those who stood behind were in breach. The signatories stood to the sides. A dragon's clauses are not hidden; they swing.",
      ironpalm: "He swung the tail behind him. I stood in front and did not move. Those behind moved.",
      ranger: "Tail. Behind it, wide arc. Kept to its flank and shot over the shoulder. Anyone who stood behind it knows by now.",
      shadowblade: "The mark swung its tail behind it, which is where I usually stand. Rude. Moved to its side, where neither end can see me. A new rule: never trust the other end.",
      pyromancer: "He swung that tail behind him and I was standing in just the wrong place, glowing! Learned to stand at his side. A little bruised. Dragons ought to announce their rear.",
      thermist: "Tail: behind him. Stay off that arc. Then the heat came and I was swinging back in, from the flank, not the rear. The rear is an unsolved problem. I gave up on it.",
      driftblade: "The tail came round behind him in a great arc and I went through the gap beside it, out the front and round again, and never behind him, because behind is where birds get caught.",
      marionettist: "The star's tail swept the upstage. I had my Doll in the wings and the cast at his sides, where the arc does not reach. (A respectable review.)",
      cleric: "The tail swept behind him and caught a friend who had wandered there. I told her to keep to his sides, and mended what I could. Next time she will listen. Next time they all will, I think.",
      grovekeeper: "The tail comes round like a storm cloud behind him. Keep to his flanks and the weather passes you by. My lot learned it the first time.",
      chronomancer: "He turns his shoulder, and the tail comes round a moment later, behind him. I was already at his side. I knew it, I think. Possibly from last time.",
      alembist: "The tail, a wide sweep behind him: stand at his flanks or his front and nowhere in the arc. The shelf behind him is not safe either, I found. Moved it.",
      threadweaver: "The tail swept his back, and anyone stitched in there came away torn. Mind the seam. My stitches stand at his flanks now.",
      tidecaller: "The tail swept the ground behind him, where nobody with sense ought to be. We kept to his front and his flanks, and the ones who wandered round his back came away rinsed. The stern of a ship is the part that swings.",
      lanternkeeper: "The tail swept the ground behind him, where nobody with sense stands. We kept to his front and his flanks, and my lanterns went there too, never at his back.",
      kenseier: "He swept the tail behind him, not before. I kept my place at his front, and the others stood to his sides. Whoever stood at his back learned the beat quickly. A tail is a second blade, and it sits where the first is not.",
      houndmaster: "Tail swung behind him, wide. Kept to his flank and sent the dogs the same way. Anyone who stood at his back found out. Hounds too, if you're careless.",
    } },
    'Magma Burst': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The marks came up over the others, not me. I held his front while each took their own patch of floor and stepped off their own pool when it struck. A quiet job, for once: keeping him pointed away.",
      gloomsworn: "Marks rose over every head but mine. Each of them took a place apart and left the pool when it burst, and no one needed me for it. A rare quiet job.",
      thresher: "A ring rose over each of us but our knight, and every one wanted its own ground. I walked out to mine, apart from the others, let it fall, then stepped off the pool it left. Poor footing there, so I tiptoed on, whistling.",
      bloodreaver: "Little circles of fire over my friends, and each leaves a puddle of molten rock. I stayed with the big lizard and let them handle theirs. Step out of your puddle, fast. I kept grinning and let them hop.",
      runeguard: "Marks over each of us, save the one in front. Each takes a patch of their own, apart, then leaves the pool behind. A beautifully orderly torment. I numbered the pools, briefly, before they cooled.",
      gorgemaw: "The marks were little canapés over my friends' heads, each landing in a puddle of sauce. I did not eat one. Each took their own plate and left it afterwards. Hot, hot, hot.",
      oathbroker: "Each marked party owes a circle of his own, and the pool remains after. Do not share, and do not stand in your own debt. Each liability is several, not joint.",
      ironpalm: "The marks came over the others. They parted. Each stood alone, and each stepped out of what they left.",
      ranger: "Marks over the others. Fanned out, one patch each. Stepped out of the pools after. I stayed on the dragon. Lucky draw.",
      shadowblade: "The marks went over everyone but me, and I spent the moment thinking how nice that was. Each of them took their own patch, then left the pool behind. Nobody looked at me. Heaven.",
      pyromancer: "Marks over my head, which I took as a compliment! Each of us needed our own patch of floor, and each leaves a pool of molten rock. Stepped out of mine quickly.",
      thermist: "Marks. Apart, each alone. Then the pool underfoot: out of it at once! I was in mine too long, boiled over, and had to be mended. Moral: move before the experiment finishes.",
      driftblade: "The marks came and I swept to my own patch at the far edge, and the others fanned out, and when it struck I was already moving, out of the pool and through the gap and round again.",
      marionettist: "Each of us got our own spotlight, and each spotlight leaves a hot patch on the boards. The cast fanned out wide, and left their marks at once. (My Doll was spared the lights.)",
      cleric: "The marks fell on my young ones, each on their own. I told them: apart, and out of the pool you leave behind. They did, bless them, with only one scorched boot among them.",
      grovekeeper: "The marks came like scattered seed, one over each of my lot. Everyone took their own ground, and when it fell they stepped out of the molten patch.",
      chronomancer: "The marks appear, and a moment later they land, pool and all. I had stepped off mine by then, and I will again.",
      alembist: "Marks over each of us, then a puddle of molten rock: apart from each other for the mark, then off the puddle at once. Keep the flasks well apart, too.",
      threadweaver: "Each mark wanted its own seam, so I cut the threads before they snapped and tied them again after we regrouped. And off the molten patch.",
      tidecaller: "Marks rose over every head but the knight's. Each of us walked off to a patch of floor of our own, away from the rest, and stepped out of the pool the moment it broke, for the pools stay. A quiet shore is a safe one.",
      lanternkeeper: "Marks rose over every head but the knight's. Each of us went to a patch of floor alone, and stepped out of the pool the instant it burst, since the pools stay. I made sure no lantern of mine sat in one.",
      kenseier: "Marks rose over the others and left me alone. I kept his head turned away from them while they each went to their own patch and stepped clear of the pool as it landed. A quiet duty: only holding his eyes.",
      houndmaster: "Marks over the others, none on me. Kept his head turned from them while they each went to their own patch and stepped clear of the pool. Dull job, a necessary one.",
    } },
    'Cinder Feast': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A blue ring settled on me and the whole line crowded in, since it follows where I stand. He bit down on it, then again, then again. I kept my feet planted so the ring stayed put, and the healer worked in the gaps between. Nobody broke the ring.",
      gloomsworn: "The ring went blue about my feet, and the others came in under it, close as mourners. He bit down more than once. I stayed where I was so no one would have to chase me. It hurt in turns, which is the kind I prefer.",
      thresher: "A blue ring on our knight, and everyone in under it, tidy as dancers in a circle. It bites more than once, a hum apart, so stay inside through every bite and let the healer mend in the pauses. Nobody leaves mid-dance, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "A blue ring on ME, and the whole party crowded in! He chewed on us bite after bite and I stood my ground so the ring stayed put. A friend missing from the crowd makes it hurt worse for everyone. The healer topped us up between.",
      runeguard: "A blue ring was drawn about my feet, and it follows me, so everyone must be inside it and I must not wander. The bites come in a series, each a beat after the last, and the mending belongs in the pauses between.",
      gorgemaw: "A great blue plate set before me, and the other guests crowded round it. He bit into it a mouthful at a time. I stayed seated so the plate stayed put, everyone stayed in, and the healer poured between courses. Rich, if repetitive.",
      oathbroker: "The terms: a blue ring about the holder, owed by all of us in common and payable in instalments a beat apart. I did not move, so the ring did not, and every co-signatory remained within it. Our healer settled the balance between instalments.",
      ironpalm: "He spoke a short phrase to me and to everyone close, again and again. We gathered in the blue ring and stayed. I did not walk. Between phrases came the healer's answer. Each pause, we answered together.",
      ranger: "Blue ring on the shield-bearer. Everyone in it, no one at the edge. It bites in a string, a beat between each. He stayed planted and so did we. Healer filled every gap.",
      shadowblade: "The ring went blue around our knight and everyone crowded in, which I resent on principle. He bit down in turns, so I stayed put in the crowd and let the healer work between the bites. Even I can be shared among friends.",
      pyromancer: "A blue ring around our knight, and the whole company crushed in beside him! I hated standing still, but the bites came one after another, and a missing friend made it worse for all of us. I cast from inside the crowd, and the healer mended between.",
      cleric: "A blue ring on our knight, and I called everyone in under it before the first bite. It bit again and again, a breath apart. I laid the light on them in every pause, and not one of us stepped out.",
      grovekeeper: "The ring went blue on the knight and my lot crowded in like saplings in a hedge. It bites in a row with a pause between, so I sowed the mending in each pause. No one leaves the hedge until the last bite has fallen.",
      chronomancer: "The ring is blue and follows our knight, and the first bite is never the last. They come on a steady beat. I healed on the off-beat between them, and we all stayed in until the last one rang. I will have counted them correctly, naturally.",
      alembist: "A heavy bite, repeated, centred on the knight's ring. Everyone inside the blue ring, no gaps, and a measured dose in each pause rather than one big bottle after. Label on the shelf: do not leave early. A few corks popped, as intended.",
      thermist: "Blue ring on the knight, all of us inside, nobody wandering. Then the bites came, one after another, and I ran hot with each. Each time I stepped in again, close and steady. The healer mended in the gaps, and I stayed in till the last.",
      driftblade: "The ring on our knight went blue and I had to stand in it with everyone, which I loathe, and he bit down once and again and again, so I did not slip out through any gap, I stayed to the last bite and then swept away through the smoke.",
      marionettist: "A blue spotlight fixed on the star, and the whole company crowded into it for the long bow. He bit down on the scene, bite after bite, so no one exits until the last. The healer works in the pauses. (The Doll watched from the wings, safe.)",
      threadweaver: "A blue ring around our knight, and I drew every thread in close, no slack and no stragglers. He bit at it over and over, so I mended in the gaps between and kept every stitch inside until the last one landed.",
      tidecaller: "A blue ring on our knight, a pool for all of us to stand in, and he bit into it again and again. I raised the swell in each lull between the bites, and kept my feet in the ring until the last had come. A rising tide does not leave early.",
      lanternkeeper: "The ring went blue around our knight and followed him. Four lights, one ring, and all of us inside it. He bit down more than once, so I topped up the wicks in each gap and stayed put until the last. The ring only moves if he does.",
      kenseier: "He bit down on a blue ring about me, and the whole party stood in it. Not one stroke but a series, each bite on its own beat with a rest between. I held my place so the ring held its place, and the healer worked in the rests.",
      houndmaster: "Blue ring round the shield-man, and all of us in it, dogs and all. Then the big lad bit down, bite after bite. Stayed in till the last, sat the dogs on the spot, and the healer mended between.",
    } },
    'Dragonfall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He rose, and embers drew a line across the floor from the sky to a scorched pit. The line and the circle at its end were what hurt. I held the ground, clear of both. I refuse to be impressed by a falling dragon. I am a little impressed.",
      gloomsworn: "He rose out of sight, and the embers drew his road to a pit. The road hurts, and so does the ring at its end. I stood clear of both and watched him arrive on nothing.",
      thresher: "Up he went, and the embers drew his road to one of the pits, the ring showing which. I stepped off the road and well clear of the pit, and let him go down it alone. His appointment, not mine, and what a tidy road to it.",
      bloodreaver: "He dives! A line of embers from the edge, and a great red circle where he lands. I wanted to meet him there. I stayed off the line and out of the circle, for once, and bled elsewhere.",
      runeguard: "Embers draw a line from the edge of the crater to one of three scorched pits, and a ring blooms on the pit he has chosen. Off the line and out of the ring. The line is the sentence; the ring, its punctuation.",
      gorgemaw: "The beast arrived by air, as a roast should: a trail of embers, a plate set at one of three pits. I stepped aside for the dish and kept off the plate. Served hot.",
      oathbroker: "The Dragonfall is a summons served in the air, the place of delivery named only as he commits: one of three pits. The flight line is part of the instrument. Clear of both. The jurisdiction was the sky.",
      ironpalm: "He came from the sky along a line of fire. I watched for the ring where he would land. Then I stepped aside, to one side of the line.",
      ranger: "Dive. Ember line from the rim to one of three pits. The ring shows which. Moved out sideways, away from both. First flying thing I've wanted to shoot.",
      shadowblade: "The mark flew, and I loved it. A line of embers across the floor and a ring where it would land. I waited out of both and slipped back in the moment it touched down.",
      pyromancer: "He flew right over my head and I gasped! The embers drew a line and then a ring blossomed, the colour of trouble, at one of three pits. I stepped out of both. A grand entrance. I took notes.",
      thermist: "Dive: hot line, hot ring. Out of both, to the side. Which pit, then go. I stood in the ring once and burnt. Observed: do not.",
      driftblade: "He came down the line of embers and I went across it, through the gap, away from the pit he'd chosen, and out the far side before he landed, and round again, still aloft.",
      marionettist: "The star made his entrance from the flies, along a line of footlights and onto one of three marks. The cast cleared the line and the mark. (A grand entrance. A better exit.)",
      cleric: "The dragon dove, and I watched the line of embers and the ring that bloomed. Off the line, out of the ring, I called, and we all were, and I was prouder than a priest ought to be.",
      grovekeeper: "He came like a storm out of a clear sky, along a line of embers. I read where the ring bloomed and moved my lot out of both. Weather you can read, is weather you can leave.",
      chronomancer: "The line is drawn well before he comes, and the ring blooms on the pit he picks. I had stepped off the line at the first note. For once.",
      alembist: "A dive: a line of embers, a ring at the end of it, and three pits to pick from. Off the line and out of the ring. Label the pits: left, middle, hot.",
      threadweaver: "He cut across the crater like a pulled thread, and the ring marked where he'd stitch it down. I got every stitch off the line and out of the ring.",
      tidecaller: "He climbed out of sight, and a trail of embers drew his road across the floor to the ring where he would land. The road hurts and so does the ring, so we stood clear of both. A storm read early can be sailed round.",
      lanternkeeper: "Up he went out of sight, and a trail of embers drew his road across the floor to the ring where he would come down. Road and ring both hurt. I walked the party out of the way of both and lit nothing there.",
      kenseier: "He rose, and embers drew a line across the floor from the sky to a scorched pit. The line and the circle at its end were what hurt. I stayed clear of both. Even a falling dragon announces where he will land, which is more than most do.",
      houndmaster: "Ember line from the rim down to one of three pits, ring at the far end. Stayed clear of both, sideways, and called the dogs off the line. He tells you where he'll land. Be elsewhere.",
    } },
    'Searing Descent': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He came down, and then breathed across the floor in three sweeps, one after another. I took the line out of the landing, then stepped into the sweep that had already gone by. Behind a spent blow is the firmest footing.",
      gloomsworn: "He landed, and fire swept out from his feet in three blows, side to side. I left the ring, then stood where the first blow had already passed. The dead taught me that the place just struck is the quietest. I keep to it.",
      thresher: "He lands, then the fire reaps across the crater in three swaths, one behind the other. I left the ring, then followed the scythe: whatever it had already cut was safe to stand in. Reap behind the blade.",
      bloodreaver: "He landed and BREATHED, three great sweeps in a row! I danced out of the ring, then into the patch the fire had already burned through. Never run from a fire to the side it is going to. Run to where it was.",
      runeguard: "A ring, and then a fan of fire drawn in three parts, to be read in order. Leave the ring, then take the part that goes off first and stand there once it has. A spent line is the safest page in the book.",
      gorgemaw: "He came down and served three courses of fire, one after another, sweeping the table. I left the ring, then sat down in the course that had been served already. The first dish is always the safest seat once it is gone.",
      oathbroker: "A second clause after the landing: three sweeps of fire, one by one, all drawn at the start. Clear of the ring, then into whichever clause had been served already. A spent clause binds no one.",
      ironpalm: "He landed, then breathed in three sweeps. I left the ring, and when the first sweep had gone I stepped into where it had been. The place just struck is calm.",
      ranger: "Down on the spot, then three breaths of fire swept across, one after another. Out of the ring first. Then into the sweep already gone. Watched the first one's edge, not its middle.",
      shadowblade: "He landed, then fire swept out in three cuts. I left the ring and slipped behind the first sweep as it went by. A spent blow leaves a dark corner behind it. I like dark corners.",
      pyromancer: "He landed and BREATHED FIRE in three sweeps, which is the closest anyone has come to out-doing me! I ran out of the ring, then into the part the first sweep had already blackened. Burnt ground is the only safe ground. Delicious.",
      thermist: "Landing, then three sweeps of heat in turn. Reading: the first sweep leaves the coolest patch behind it. Leave the ring, then stand in the part already swept. Conclusion: hot first, then cold.",
      driftblade: "He landed and fire swept across in three strokes, and I went out of the ring and then slid in behind the first sweep as it left, riding the gap it made. The wind goes where the fire has been.",
      marionettist: "After the entrance, three acts of fire, one after another, side to side. Out of the ring, and then onto the part of the stage the first act had already burned. (A finished act is the safest set.)",
      cleric: "He landed, and the fire swept across in three turns. I called everyone out of the ring and then into the part the first sweep had already passed. The place just struck is still, and a still place is a kindness.",
      grovekeeper: "He lands and the fire sweeps out in three bands, one after another, like a scythe on a field, dear. Out of the ring, then onto the band already cut. Where the fire has been once, it will not be again.",
      chronomancer: "The ring, then three sweeps in order, each close behind the last. I left the ring on the first beat and stood where the first sweep had gone by. I had not counted a ruin before; it keeps good time.",
      alembist: "After the landing, a reaction in three parts, one after another. Out of the ring, then into the part already reacted. Label it: spent. Spent is safe. Mostly. I was right, mostly.",
      threadweaver: "He landed and fire ran across in three long stitches, one after another. We left the ring, then stood in the seam where the first had already run. A stitched line does not tear twice.",
      tidecaller: "He came down, and the fire swept out in three waves, one behind the other. We left the ring and then stood in the trough the first wave had already passed. The slack water is always behind the wave.",
      lanternkeeper: "He landed and fire swept across in three turns, side to side. We left the ring, and I set my lanterns in the part the first sweep had already passed, where the dark was safe. Follow the fire's tail, not its head.",
      kenseier: "He landed, and then the fire swept in three strokes, and I counted them. Out of the ring, then into the line the first stroke had cut. The place just struck is where the blade is not.",
      houndmaster: "Came down, then fire swept across in three goes. Out of the ring, then into where the first sweep had been. Dogs to heel, close behind it. Safe behind the fire, never in front.",
    } },
    'Twin Pass': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Two lines of embers that cross. He flew the first without landing and came round on the second, close behind it. The first road is safe once he has flown it, so I crossed it and kept clear of the second and its ring.",
      gloomsworn: "Two roads of ember, crossing. The first he only flew over, the second he landed on, and hard on its heels. Once the first was flown I stood in it, clear of the second and its ring. A road already burned is quiet ground.",
      thresher: "Two roads of embers, crossing each other. He only flies the first and lands on the second right behind it. Once the first is cut it is safe to stand on, so I stepped over it, clear of the second. A pretty two-step, if you keep moving.",
      bloodreaver: "Two lines that cross! The first he only passes over, the second ends in a ring and a landing, hot on its heels. The moment he'd flown the first I ran through it, clear of the second. A cheat, I call that, and I loved it.",
      runeguard: "Two lines drawn, crossing, the second following close behind the first. The first is only a pass; the second ends in a landing. Once the first is flown it is safe to cross, and the second must be left. The second line is what a careless reader would miss.",
      gorgemaw: "Two courses from the air that cross the table: the first only flies over, the second lands, right behind it. Once the first had passed I crossed it, clear of the second and its ring. The second was the better.",
      oathbroker: "Two writs, served close together and crossing. The first is a flyover with no landing; the second carries the ring. Once the first was served it bound no one, so I crossed it, clear of the second. Sequence matters.",
      ironpalm: "Two lines, crossing. The first passed over; the second landed, close behind. When the first had gone I stepped across it, clear of the second and its ring.",
      ranger: "Two lines, crossing. First he only passes. Second he lands, right behind. Once the first is flown it's safe ground: cross it, clear of the second. Do not stop after the first.",
      shadowblade: "The mark made two passes across each other and landed on the second, hard behind the first. Once the first was flown I slipped across it, clear of the second and its ring. A spent road hides nothing, and nothing hides in it.",
      pyromancer: "Twice, and the lines CROSS! The first is only a pass and the second lands, right behind it. The moment the first was flown I ran through it, clear of the second. Very nearly graceful.",
      thermist: "Two passes that cross, the second close behind the first: first only over, second down. Once the first has gone it is cool to stand on, so cross it and read the second. I stopped on the wrong side once.",
      driftblade: "Two lines crossing and I cut across the first as it went out, through the cooled lane and over to the clear patch, and round before the second lit. It was glorious. The wind crosses what it has already burned.",
      marionettist: "A double entrance, the paths crossing: the star flies the first without stopping, then lands on the second right behind it. Once he had passed, the first was safe to stand on; the second was not. (Quick exits, all round.)",
      cleric: "Two lines in the sky, crossing. The first he only flew over; the second he landed on, close behind. Once the first was flown I called everyone across it, clear of the second and its ring. A road already burned is a kindness.",
      grovekeeper: "Two gusts that cross, one after the other, and the second is the one that lands. Once the first has blown past, the ground it crossed is safe. Step over it, clear of the second. Move twice.",
      chronomancer: "Two lines, crossing, two notes in quick succession: the second lands soon after the first has gone over. I crossed the first as soon as it was flown, and cleared the second. This time I did.",
      alembist: "A double dive, the lines crossing: the first is only a pass, the second ends in a landing, close behind it. Once the first is spent it is safe. Cross it, clear of the second. Label both lines. I did, until it burned.",
      threadweaver: "Two long threads that cross the crater, the first only passing through, the second tying down at a pit soon after. Once the first thread had run, we stepped across it and clear of the other. No dawdling.",
      tidecaller: "Two roads of ember crossing each other: he flew the first and came down on the second soon after. Once the first had passed we crossed its wake, slack as still water, and kept clear of the second.",
      lanternkeeper: "Two roads of ember that cross: he flew over the first and came down on the second soon after. Once the first was flown we crossed it and left the second, and I set my lantern beyond both, where we would be after.",
      kenseier: "He flew two ember lines that cross: the first overhead, no landing, then round onto the second soon after. Once the first was flown I crossed it, and one clean step took me clear of the second. The second step is the one a lazy swordsman forgets.",
      houndmaster: "Two ember lines crossing. First he only passes. Second he lands, right after. Once the first is flown, it's safe: cross it and clear of the second. Dogs don't know there's a second, so I called them twice.",
    } },
    'Talon Dive': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He dived at me. The embers ran from the rim to where I stood and the ring waited at my boots. I led him off across the empty floor, out of the ring, and the others kept off the line. Fell back, on purpose.",
      gloomsworn: "He dived at me, as they do. The embers ran to where I stood and the ring waited for me. I walked out of it, the others kept off the line, and he came down on empty stone. I am used to being the thing aimed at.",
      thresher: "He dives at whoever holds him. The embers ran to our knight's boots and the ring waited there, so the knight walked him off and the rest of us stood clear of the line. Sheaves are cut where they stand; knights can walk.",
      bloodreaver: "He came for ME! The embers ran straight to my boots and the ring sat there waiting. I like being wanted. I dragged him off across the empty floor, the others kept clear of the line, and he landed on nothing. Poor fellow.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a road of embers from the rim to my own feet, the ring laid there at the start. The answer: walk him clear of the party before it fills, and let the others keep off the line. Predicted, and flattering besides.",
      gorgemaw: "He dives at the one who holds him, so the dish was set before me: a line of embers ending at my boots. I carried it to the empty end of the table, the others kept off the line, and he landed on a clean plate.",
      oathbroker: "A writ served on the holder of the field: the line ended at my feet, and the ring was already drawn there. I led him to open floor and the others kept off the line. Service accepted, elsewhere.",
      ironpalm: "He dived at me. The embers said so. I walked out of the ring and led him away from the others, who stayed off the line. He landed on nothing. I did not smile.",
      ranger: "Dive at the shield-bearer. Line from the rim to his boots, ring where he stood. He walked clear, I stayed off the line, and shot as soon as the dust fell. Clean.",
      shadowblade: "The mark chose the one in front, which is rude to everyone else. The embers ran to his feet and I stayed clear of both line and ring. He landed on air. I was at his flank before the dust fell.",
      pyromancer: "He dived at our knight, and I was almost jealous! A road of embers to the boots and a ring waiting. The knight walked it away from the rest of us and I kept off the line. Show-off. Mine will be grander.",
      thermist: "Dive: aimed at whoever holds him. Line from the rim to the boots, ring on the spot. Reading: hot, then hotter. Step off the line, lead him away from the others. Result: he landed on a warm patch of nothing.",
      driftblade: "He dived at the one holding him, embers running to the knight's boots, and the knight slipped sideways out of the ring and I flowed out of the line, and the dragon landed where everyone had just been.",
      marionettist: "The star made his entrance at the leading man: the embers ran straight to his mark. The knight walked the spotlight off stage and the cast kept out of the line. (He landed to polite silence.)",
      cleric: "He dived at our knight. The embers showed the road and the ring, and I called to the others to keep off the line while the knight walked him away. I mended an empty crater after. A kind sort of dive.",
      grovekeeper: "He dives at whoever holds him, dear. A bright road to the knight's boots and a ring waiting at the end of it. The knight walks him off to bare ground and the rest of us keep off the road. Nothing of mine was planted there.",
      chronomancer: "The line ran to our knight's feet and the ring was laid there at the start. I knew where it would end before it began, and so did the knight. He walked, we stood clear, and the dragon landed on old footprints.",
      alembist: "A dive aimed at the one who holds him: the embers end at his boots. The knight walks him off to open floor, everyone else keeps off the line. I would have labelled the ring, but nobody was in it.",
      threadweaver: "He dived along a thread that ended at our knight's boots. The knight carried the whole length of it off to open floor and the rest of us kept off the line. Not one stitch of us was inside the ring.",
      tidecaller: "He dives at the one who holds him, a road of embers straight to the knight's boots. The knight led it out of the ring the way a swimmer leads a current off the shore, and the rest of us stayed off the line.",
      lanternkeeper: "He dived at the one who holds him: embers to our knight's boots, the ring waiting. The knight led him off into open floor, and I kept my lanterns off the line. Light follows the one he hunts.",
      kenseier: "He dived at me. The embers drew the line to my feet and the ring was there from the start, so there was no hurry: one clean step out of it, the others off the line. He landed late, on nothing. Each dive had the same count.",
      houndmaster: "Dived at the shield-man. Line to his boots, ring on the spot. He walked off clear, we stayed off the line, dogs held back. Landed on bare rock.",
    } },
    'Pounce': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He crouched at me and a ring of fire settled round my boots, following until it held still. I walked out of it, well off the others, and he came down on bare stone. Then I led him round his own fire. Withdrew in good order.",
      gloomsworn: "He coiled for me, and a ring of fire fixed itself about my feet. I left it a breath before he sprang and let him land on nothing. The floor burned where he came down. I walked him away from it. The rest kept off the rim.",
      thresher: "He crouches at our knight, and a ring is drawn at the knight's boots, following until the last. The knight stepped out, he came down on bare ground, and the ground burned after. We kept off the rim and cut at his flank once he was led clear.",
      bloodreaver: "He sprang AT me! A ring round my boots, and I stood in it, grinning, and took the landing. The floor burned where he came down, so I marched him out of his own fire with my friends well off the rim. A fine welcome.",
      runeguard: "A ring settles at the holder's feet, follows, then is fixed in place. Step out as it fixes, or take it, but lead him off the fire he leaves where he lands, and let the others keep off the rim. Noted in the margin, and underlined.",
      gorgemaw: "He crouched over me like a waiter with a covered dish, a ring of fire set at my feet. I stepped off the plate and he landed on nothing. The floor stayed hot there, so I carried the meal to a cooler table, the others off the rim.",
      oathbroker: "A notice served on the holder: a ring about his feet, then a leap into it. The holder may vacate before it fixes. I did, and he landed on an empty lot, which smouldered. I led him off it. Trespassers keep off the rim.",
      ironpalm: "He crouched. A ring followed me and held. I stepped out of it and he landed on bare stone, and the floor burned there after. I led him off the heat, the others clear of the rim.",
      ranger: "He crouched for the shield-bearer, the ring following him until it set. Out of the ring, and he landed on nothing, and the ground burned after. I kept off the rim and shot from the flank once he had been walked clear of it.",
      shadowblade: "The mark crouched for the one in front. The ring follows, then fixes, then he drops. I was never in it. The knight stepped out, the mark landed on a floor that burned, and I came in at his flank once he was walked off the fire.",
      pyromancer: "He crouched, and for one glorious moment I thought it was at me! It was the knight, a ring of fire at the knight's boots. The knight stepped out and he landed in a patch that burned a good while. Not my fire, for once. I kept off the rim.",
      thermist: "A ring on the holder, following, then still. He springs into it and the floor there stays hot. Step out before it sets, lead him off the heat, others off the rim. Reading: one very warm patch, and a very cool me beside it.",
      driftblade: "He crouched and a ring settled at the knight's boots. The knight slid sideways out of it, I flowed round the rim, and he came down on stone that burned after. The knight led him off the fire. Wind does not burn.",
      marionettist: "The star crouched for the leading man, a ring of fire at his mark. The leading man exited stage left; the star landed on an empty mark, and the boards burned. The cast kept off the rim, and the star was walked off the fire.",
      cleric: "He crouched at our knight, and a ring of fire fixed at the knight's feet. I called the rest back from the rim; the knight stepped clear and he landed on bare stone that burned after. The knight walked him off the fire.",
      grovekeeper: "He crouched at the knight, dear, and a ring of fire fixed itself at the boots. The knight stepped out, he came down on nothing, and the soil where he landed burned. We kept to the edge, then followed him away from the scorch.",
      chronomancer: "A ring follows the knight, then holds still: that is the moment. The knight steps out, the dragon comes down on empty stone, and the floor burns where he landed. I knew where to stand a moment before. Off the rim, off the fire.",
      alembist: "He crouched at the one who holds him, a ring of fire at the boots. Step out as it fixes, lead him off the heat he leaves on landing, keep the others off the rim. I chalked the rim for next time.",
      threadweaver: "He sprang along no thread at all: a ring of fire fixed at the knight's boots and he came down in it. The knight stepped out and the rest of us kept off the edge. The patch that burned afterwards, I left unmended and well away.",
      tidecaller: "He crouched for the knight and a ring settled at the knight's boots like a tide pool. The knight stepped out as it set, he fell into the empty pool, and it burned. We kept off the rim and the knight led him out of the heat.",
      lanternkeeper: "He crouched at the one who holds him, a ring of fire at our knight's boots. The knight stepped out, he landed in it, and the floor burned a while. I kept my lanterns off the rim and hung the next one where the knight led him.",
      kenseier: "He crouched, and a ring followed me and held. One step out of it, no hurry, and he came down on nothing, and the floor burned where he landed. I led him round his own fire. Each spring had the same count.",
      houndmaster: "Dragon crouched at the shield-man, ring of fire at his boots, following, then fixed. Shield-man stepped out, he landed on bare rock, floor burned after. Dogs held back off the rim. Walk him off the heat before the dogs go in.",
    } },
    'Aftershock': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "After the landing the ground rang outward from his feet in rings. The quiet was at his side, so I went back to him, shield first, and the line followed me in.",
      gloomsworn: "The ground rang out from where he landed, ring after ring. Only the middle, close at his feet, was quiet. I went back in beside him, among the claws, where the dead are comfortable.",
      thresher: "After he lands, the ground rolls out from his feet in rings, like wheat before a gale. The quiet stays at the middle, hard against him. So I stepped out of the landing, then right back in beside him. In time.",
      bloodreaver: "He landed and the whole floor kicked outward from his feet! Only the ground beside him stayed still. So I stepped out of the landing, then went straight back in at his side, close as kin. A scar missed is a scar wasted, but not this one.",
      runeguard: "First the landing, then the ground rings outward from his feet, and only the quiet against his body is left unmarked. Out of the landing, then back in beside him; the margin is narrow, and so is the reward.",
      gorgemaw: "After the landing the floor rang out in rings, a second course I was not ready for. Only the head of the table, hard against him, stayed calm. Out of the landing, then in to his side. A large helping. Eat close.",
      oathbroker: "After the landing, a second clause: the floor rings out from his feet, and only the ground at his side is exempt. We cleared the landing, then returned to his side at once. Exemptions are narrow, and always at the source.",
      ironpalm: "He landed, and the ground rang outward. The quiet was beside him. I stepped out of the landing and back in at his side, and the rings went past. Close is safe.",
      ranger: "After he lands the ground rings out from him. Quiet only against his side. Out of the landing, then straight back in. I stood closer than I like to, and the rings went by.",
      shadowblade: "The mark landed and the ground rang outward like a struck bell. The quiet is right up against him, which is where I like to be anyway. Out of the landing, back in at his flank. Delicious.",
      pyromancer: "He landed and the floor rang out in rings, which is MY trick! Only the patch at his feet stayed calm. So: out of the landing, then in again beside him, closer than I normally dare. My nerves held, which is rarer.",
      thermist: "Landing, then rings running out of him, hot. The quiet is right by his feet. Step out of the landing, then in again at once. Reading: a cold spot in the middle. Conclusion: stand in the middle.",
      driftblade: "He landed and the floor rang out in rings, and I went out of the landing and straight back in at his side, one ring ahead of the next, and they went past me like wind past a stone.",
      marionettist: "After the entrance the stage shook outward in rings from his mark, and the quiet was only right beside him. Exit the landing, return to his side on the next cue. (Close to the star is a place of safety. I was surprised too.)",
      cleric: "When he landed, the ground rang out in rings, and only the floor against him was still. I called everyone out of the landing and then straight back in beside him. Closer than anyone would choose, but that was where the quiet was.",
      grovekeeper: "He lands and the ground ripples out from his feet like a stone in a pond, my dears. Only the soil right by him stays still. Step out of the landing, then in again beside him. A strange place to be safe.",
      chronomancer: "The landing, then the rings: the ground rolls out from his feet a moment later. The quiet is against him. I left the landing and was back at his side before the first ring. This time I counted it right.",
      alembist: "After the landing, rings run out of him through the floor. The one still place is beside him. Out of the landing, then in again at his side, quickly. Label the spot: do not leave. Close is the cure.",
      threadweaver: "He landed and the ground rang out in rings from his feet. Only the cloth beside him stayed flat. We stepped out of the landing and drew in against him, close as a stitch. A tight seam is a safe one.",
      tidecaller: "After the landing the ground rolled out from him in rings, like a wave leaving a stone. Only the water right against him stayed slack. We stepped clear of the landing and then in beside him. Slack water is always by the rock.",
      lanternkeeper: "After the landing the ground rang out in rings from his feet, and only the floor beside him stayed still. We left the landing, then came back in close, and I set my lanterns at his side, where the rings could not reach.",
      kenseier: "He landed, and then the floor rang outward from his feet, and I counted. Out of the landing, then in again at his side, before the first ring. The quiet is always at the source of the stroke.",
      houndmaster: "After he landed the ground rang out in rings. Quiet only against him. Out of the landing, then in tight at his side. Dogs kept close, no lagging. His flank is the safe place.",
    } },
    'The Roost': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He perched on the rim and shook his burning scales onto the floor, three at a time, and two whelps came down with them. The line kept moving off the scales and put the whelps down. Standing still would have been the first mistake.",
      gloomsworn: "He perched high and shed fiery scales in threes, two whelps dropping with them. We never stopped moving off the scales, and the whelps were dead before he came down. Idle hands are for graves.",
      thresher: "He sat up on the rim and shook his burning scales loose, always one patch beneath somebody, so I kept walking. Two small whelps came down with them, and I sent them to the far shore before he dropped. Early, but their names were on the list.",
      bloodreaver: "The big lizard sat on the rim and rained burning scales on us, and two little ones came to bite. Good fun. Keep moving, kill the little ones before he drops, or they go off.",
      runeguard: "He perches high and the scales fall, three at a time, always one under one of us. Keep moving, and put the whelps down before he comes to the ground, or they burst. A short text, with a very short deadline.",
      gorgemaw: "The beast sat on the rim and shook his burning crumbs onto the floor, and sent two small portions to nibble at us. Better to eat the portions than let them cool and burst. Crunchy.",
      oathbroker: "The Roost is a long recess with penalties: scales fall on named tiles, one under each party in turn, and two small bailiffs come to collect. They burst if they outlive the recess.",
      ironpalm: "He sat on the rim, high. Fire fell in threes. Two small ones came at us. I stepped aside each time, and broke the small ones before he came down.",
      ranger: "Perched on the rim. Scales fall in threes, one under someone. Two whelps. Kept moving, shot the whelps down before he drops.",
      shadowblade: "The mark perched on the rim and wept fire, three tiles at a time, and one was always under somebody. The whelps were rude. I put them down before he came to the ground.",
      pyromancer: "He sat on the rim and flaked fire onto the floor like a bonfire shedding sparks, and I loved it, and kept moving! The two little dragons must be put down before he lands. My boots are a disgrace.",
      thermist: "Scales fall hot, in threes. Move off them. Whelps bite: put them down. They burst if left. I left one once. Now with an empty sleeve.",
      driftblade: "He sat on the rim and burning scales fell in threes and I wove between them, round them, through the gaps, and cut down the little dragons on the way past.",
      marionettist: "The star took the balcony and showered the stalls with fire, three at a time, one on someone each time. Two small understudies came on. The cast must dismiss them before he descends.",
      cleric: "He sat on the rim and let fire rain on us, always one patch on one of us, and the whelps harried them. Keep moving, and put the little ones down; I shouted it until I was hoarse.",
      grovekeeper: "It rained fire from the rim, three patches at a time, and I had my lot keep moving through it like walking in a hailstorm, dear. The whelps are saplings of fire: pull them before he lands.",
      chronomancer: "He shakes, and the scales fall just after, three at a time, always one under somebody. I was moving at the first. The whelps burst at the end if they linger, so I put them down early.",
      alembist: "The Roost: burning scales in threes, two whelps, a recess of some length. Keep moving, put the whelps down before he lands, or they burst. Shelve with the scorch kit. I have a scorch kit now.",
      threadweaver: "The scales fell like loose pins, three at a time, always one over one of mine. Keep moving, keep the whelps down; they burst if they are still there when he comes to the ground.",
      tidecaller: "He perched on the rim and shook burning scales down in threes, always one over someone, and two whelps came with them. We kept walking off the scales and put the whelps down before he dropped. I fed the water small and often while it rained.",
      lanternkeeper: "He perched on the rim and shook burning scales down in threes, always one over someone, with two whelps alongside. We kept walking off the scales and put the whelps down before he dropped. My lanterns trailed behind us.",
      kenseier: "He perched on the rim and shook burning scales loose, three at a time, with two whelps falling among them. We kept stepping off the scales and cut the whelps down. The one who stops to watch is the one who burns.",
      houndmaster: "He perched on the rim and shook burning scales down, three at a time, two whelps among them. Kept stepping off scales and put the dogs on the whelps. Down before he drops.",
    } },
    'Crater Boils': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The ring of floor by the rim began to glow, and it stayed that way. From then on we fought on the inner ground only. I kept the line in from the edge for the rest of the night. A smaller yard to drill in, but a yard.",
      gloomsworn: "The edge of the floor began to glow and never stopped. We were given less room after that, so I kept us to the middle for the rest of the night. I have fought in smaller places. I have lain in smaller places.",
      thresher: "The rim went from ember to flame and never cooled, so the arena is smaller for the rest of the night. I kept to the inner ground, well away from the edge. Plenty of room in the middle, once you settle in.",
      bloodreaver: "The rim boiled, and it stays boiled. A smaller room to hit him in. I like it close. Keep in from the edge, the glow bites for good.",
      runeguard: "The rim brightens and the stone along it weeps. It does not cool, so the room is smaller for the rest of the fight. Keep from the edge. I drew a new boundary on the map and pressed the pen hard.",
      gorgemaw: "The rim was set to boil, and it does not stop. The table shrank for the rest of the meal, and the edges are too hot to touch. I sat nearer the middle. Reduced, and rich.",
      oathbroker: "The Boil is a permanent amendment: the ring of floor by the rim is forfeit for the remainder. It does not cool. We keep from the edge. Property that burns is a poor lien.",
      ironpalm: "The rim boiled. It did not stop. The room was smaller after. I stayed inside, away from the edge.",
      ranger: "Rim boils. Stays boiled. Tighter ground. Kept inside the glow, away from the edge. Shorter lanes now.",
      shadowblade: "The mark cooked the rim. Nowhere to hide on the edge now, which was my favourite place. Kept to the inner floor, where he looks, and hated every step.",
      pyromancer: "The whole rim lit up like the best bonfire I have ever seen, and it stays lit! The room is smaller, and my favourite corners are lava. I stand in the middle now. Poor corners.",
      thermist: "Rim goes hot, stays hot. Smaller floor. Keep to the middle, off the edge. I stepped on it once and swung. The middle, all night.",
      driftblade: "The rim lit up, all the way round, and stayed lit, and I could not skim the wall any more, which offends me, so I flew circles in the middle instead, through everything.",
      marionettist: "The edges of the stage caught fire and stayed so. The cast was confined to the middle for the rest of the act. (The Doll, being small, did not mind.)",
      cleric: "The rim began to burn and did not stop. A smaller floor makes a closer congregation, and I kept it away from the edge, scolding gently when anyone ran too near.",
      grovekeeper: "The edge of the crater went from ember to flame, and it does not come back. Plant nothing there. My lot keep to the middle now, where the ground still takes root.",
      chronomancer: "The rim begins to glow after he stamps, and from then on it will have always been glowing. We stayed in from the edge. The room was smaller, and so was the margin.",
      alembist: "The ring of floor by the rim burns for good: stay in from the edge. Shelve nothing near it. Moved the tea. Very sad.",
      threadweaver: "The edge unpicked itself into fire, and no thread will hold there. Keep every stitch in from the rim. A smaller hem, but a cleaner one.",
      tidecaller: "The outer ring of the floor began to boil and never stopped. We kept inside it for the rest of the night, closer together, which suited me; the water goes further in a smaller pool. The rim is a shore I no longer walk.",
      lanternkeeper: "The rim of the floor began to boil and never stopped. After that we fought in a smaller circle, and I drew the lanterns in with us. The same light goes further in a smaller room. The edge is a street I no longer walk.",
      kenseier: "The ring of floor near the rim began to glow and kept glowing. After that we fought on the inner ground and never the edge. Less floor, and a floor I am used to.",
      houndmaster: "Floor by the rim started glowing and kept it. After that we fought on the inner ground, never the edge. Smaller field, shorter lanes. Keep the hounds in from the rim.",
    } },
    'Twin Breath': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A numbered sign hung over two of the others, a first and a second, and the fire followed them. Whoever carried one walked to empty floor and pointed him away from the line, the second a beat after the first. I kept my place in front and the rest stayed clear of both.",
      gloomsworn: "Two of them wore a number over their heads, and the fire hunted them in that order. Each carried it off toward empty ground, away from the rest of us, and stepped out of its path. I stayed out of the lanes. I have no number. I am glad of it.",
      thresher: "Two names on the list this time, a number floating over each, first and second. Whoever is called leads the fire off to empty ground, well clear of the party, and steps aside. The second does the same a beat later. The rest of us keep out of both lanes, dear.",
      bloodreaver: "Numbers over two heads, and the fire chases them in order! The one marked first drags it off to the empty rim, then the second does the same. I wasn't picked, shame. I kept out of the way and watched the first one get clever.",
      runeguard: "A numeral over each of two heads, and the cones take them in numeral order. The omen is on the carrier; the answer is to walk the cone to bare floor, aimed away from the party, and then leave its line. The rest of us stay clear of both sightlines.",
      gorgemaw: "Two guests were handed a numbered napkin, and the flambé went to them in that order. Each led it to the empty end of the room, pointed away from the rest, then stepped out of its path. I was neither guest and ate nothing.",
      oathbroker: "Two named parties, each marked with a numeral, and the writs served in numeral order. Each marked party leads the fire to bare floor, aimed away from the co-signatories, and then leaves its path. The unmarked keep out of both paths.",
      ironpalm: "Two of us wore numbers. The fire spoke to the first, then to the second. Each led it to empty ground, away from everyone else, and stepped out of its line. The rest of us stayed clear of both. Neither asked anything of me.",
      ranger: "Number over two heads. Fire takes them in order. Each walks it to bare ground, away from the party, then steps off the line. The rest stay out of both lanes. I was not marked. Watched the first one drag it clear.",
      shadowblade: "Two of them wore numbers like name tags, and the mark breathed on them in order. They led the fire off to empty floor and stepped out of it. I stood far from both lanes, unnumbered and unseen, which is how I prefer it.",
      pyromancer: "Numbers over two heads, and for once I was left out! The fire hunts them in order, so each marched it off to empty ground, away from the rest of us, and slipped out of its path. I stayed out of the way. Poor lonely cones.",
      thermist: "Two numbers hung over two heads. Cold plan: first carries the cone to empty ground and steps out; second does the same a beat later. Hot mistake: running at the party with it. Aim away. Everyone else, off both lines, and calm.",
      driftblade: "Two numbers over two heads and the fire running after them in order, so the first led it out toward the empty edge and slipped sideways through its path, and the second did the same a breath later, and the rest of us went wide round both.",
      marionettist: "Two of the company were handed numbered cards, and our star breathed on them in order. Each led the spotlight to the empty wings, away from the rest, and stepped out of it. The unmarked cleared both aisles. (The Doll held no card.)",
      cleric: "Two of my young ones bore a number over their heads, first and second, and the fire went after them in turn. I told each to lead it away from the rest, to bare floor, then step out of it. The others kept out of both lanes, and I with them.",
      grovekeeper: "A number over two of my lot, the first and the second, and the fire follows each. Walk it out to bare ground, away from everyone, and step out of its path. The second does likewise a beat later. The rest of us stood well clear of both.",
      chronomancer: "A number over two heads, and the cones come in that order, a beat apart. The first led its fire out to bare floor and stepped off the line, the second did the same on the following beat. Everyone else kept out of both lanes, as they will have.",
      alembist: "Two cones, two carriers, a number over each head. Dose: first carrier takes the fire to bare floor, away from the party, then leaves its line; second does the same on the next beat. The rest keep out of both. Cork the flasks.",
      threadweaver: "Numbers over two of my stitches, and a cone after each, in order. Each marked one pulled the thread out to empty floor, away from the others, and stepped aside. The rest of the hem stayed out of both lanes. A neat pair of seams.",
      tidecaller: "A number rose over two heads, first and second, and the fire followed each in turn. They walked it out over empty ground, far from the rest of us, and stepped aside as it set. The second came a long swell behind. The rest of us waited well clear.",
      lanternkeeper: "A number floated over two of us, and the fire hunted them in turn. I set my lanterns far off to the side while those two led it out to bare floor, away from everyone, and slipped from its path.",
      kenseier: "Two of the others carried a number, and the fire came for them in that order. Each led the stroke to bare floor, away from the party, and stepped out of it. The second came on its own beat, a count behind. I stayed out of both lines.",
      houndmaster: "Two of us wore numbers, and he breathed on them in order. Each walked the fire out to bare ground, away from the rest, then stepped off its line. The dogs and I stayed well off both lanes. Wide berth, the pair of them.",
    } },
    'Heart Burn': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The Heart opened and he could not be hurt. Scorch marks bloomed at the three pits one after another, and the last one marked where the fire would not reach. The line stood round it, off the mark.",
      gloomsworn: "The Heart opened and nothing could touch him. Three scorches came up in turn; the last is where the great fire does not fall. We gathered round it, never on it. The first two taught me which.",
      thresher: "His heart opened and nothing of ours would bite. Scorches came up at the pits one after another, and the last one is the only ground the great fire spares. I stood close round it, off the scorch, and waited for it to fall, humming.",
      bloodreaver: "His heart opened in his chest, and I could not touch him. Three marks, one after another, and the last is where the fire does not go. I stood by it, and not on it, and bled for nothing at all.",
      runeguard: "Three marks bloom in turn, and the third is where the great fire will not fall. Once the first two are known, the third is known; stand around it, but not on it. A proof by induction, written in embers.",
      gorgemaw: "The beast opened his heart, and we were not to touch. Three little dishes were set out in turn, and the last was the only seat the fire did not reach. We sat round it. Not on it.",
      oathbroker: "The Heart is a stay of proceedings: no claim may be made against him. The last of the three marks is the island, the only ground not forfeit. The adjudication is by observation.",
      ironpalm: "His heart opened. I could not strike. Three marks came in turn; the last is the quiet place. Stand beside it, not on it.",
      ranger: "Heart open, untouchable. Three scorches in order. The last one is where the fire stops. Stood around it, not on it. Easy to hit nothing. Hard to wait.",
      shadowblade: "The mark opened its heart, which was almost indecent. Three scorches, and the last is the only quiet ground. We stood round it, not on it.",
      pyromancer: "His heart opened and I could not burn it, which was an insult! Three burn marks bloomed, and the last was the quiet place in the fire. I stood round it, not on it, with an excellent view.",
      thermist: "Heart open, nothing to hit. Three marks, in turn. The last is the cold place. Stand round it, not on it. I stood on it once and learned the word scorch. Not that middle. The other middle.",
      driftblade: "His heart lay open and I could not cut it, so I flew. Three scorches bloomed in turn, the last the quiet place, and I was there before the fire, circling the mark.",
      marionettist: "Act the last. The star opens his heart and all the footlights go out but three, and the last of them is the only light that stays lit. The cast must stand round it, not in it.",
      cleric: "His heart opened, and no blow of ours could touch him. Three marks, and the last was the quiet place in the fire. I called everyone round it, not on it. We were all there, and I counted.",
      grovekeeper: "A fire comes over everything but the one place. Three marks, in turn, and the last is the clear ground. My lot stand round it, not on it. Know where the last is.",
      chronomancer: "Three marks, three beats, and the last is where the fire does not fall. I knew from the second mark which it would be. I stood there before it bloomed.",
      alembist: "A floor-wide fire, three marks in turn, one island. The last mark is the island; stand round it, not on it. Label the third flask. Third flask: safe. Others: not.",
      threadweaver: "The fire came over the whole floor but one hole in it. Three marks, and the last is the hole. I drew everyone to it, round the mark and not on it. Whip-stitched the edge, in my head.",
      tidecaller: "The Heart opened and he could not be hurt. Three scorches bloomed one after another, and the last marked the one island the great fire would spare. Its quiet ground is wider than the scorch, so we gathered round the mark, never on it, with the water brimming.",
      lanternkeeper: "The Heart opened and he could not be hurt. Three scorches bloomed one after another, and the last marked the one island the great fire would spare. We gathered round the mark, never on it, and I kept the wicks full against the heat.",
      kenseier: "The Heart opened and he was beyond harm. Marks of scorch bloomed at the three pits in turn; the last named the place the fire would spare. We stood about it, never on it.",
      houndmaster: "Heart opened and he was beyond harm. Scorch showed at the three pits in turn, the last where the fire spares. Stood around it, not on it. Hard to wait. Easy to be wrong.",
    } },
    'Cinder Run': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He skimmed the crater and came down on each mark in turn, all of it drawn before the first landing. The middle of the floor was on none of them. I told the line to hold the middle and watch the order fill.",
      gloomsworn: "He ran the crater and landed on every mark in turn, the whole road drawn first. I stood in the middle, which none of them touched. A watch post in the centre of a storm. I have held worse.",
      thresher: "He skims the crater and lands on each scorched mark in turn, and all of it is drawn at the start, lines and rings together. The middle is on none of them. I stayed there and watched the sheaves fall.",
      bloodreaver: "Landing after landing, one on each scorch mark, all laid out on the floor before the first! I want to meet every one. But the middle of the crater is on none of them, so I waited there and let him come round.",
      runeguard: "Every line and ring of the run is drawn at once, the landings in their order, and the middle of the crater lies on none of them. Hold the middle, and watch which ring fills first.",
      gorgemaw: "A tasting menu: he comes down on each mark in turn, and the whole menu is drawn on the floor before the first plate. The centre of the table was served nothing. I sat there, the best seat in the crater.",
      oathbroker: "A schedule of landings, each on its own mark, all filed at the outset. The middle of the crater is named in none of them. We held the middle and watched the order, and the schedule was met without us.",
      ironpalm: "He ran the crater and came down on each mark. The floor showed all of it from the start. The middle was on none of them. I stood in the middle. It was enough.",
      ranger: "Landings on every mark, all drawn first. The centre of the crater is on none of them. Held the centre, shot whenever he stood, watched the order fill. Good ground.",
      shadowblade: "The mark ran the crater and landed on each scorch in turn, and it all showed first. The middle was on none of them, so that is where I stood: the one place prey never looks.",
      pyromancer: "He skims the whole crater and comes down on each burn mark in turn, all of it drawn before the first! I stood in the middle, the one patch he ignores, and applauded each landing. Best seat in the house, and free.",
      thermist: "Run: a landing per mark, lines and rings drawn at the start. The middle is on none of them. Cold spot, mathematically. I stood in it and logged every landing. Readings: loud.",
      driftblade: "He swept the crater and came down on each mark in turn, and I stayed in the middle, the one place the wind goes through, and watched him go past me on every side and never land where I stood.",
      marionettist: "The star's grand finale: an entrance on each mark in turn, all of them chalked out before the curtain. The centre of the stage was left alone. The cast stood in it and took the applause.",
      cleric: "He swept round the crater and came down on every mark in turn. It was drawn on the floor from the start, and the middle was on none of it. We gathered there, and I mended in peace between his landings.",
      grovekeeper: "He comes down on each mark in turn, my dears, and it is all drawn out before the first. The middle of the crater is on none of them. Stand in the middle, and let the storm go round.",
      chronomancer: "A landing on every mark, all drawn before the first. I watched which ring filled first and the one after it. The middle was on none of them. This time I stood there from the start.",
      alembist: "Landings in order, each mark drawn before the first. Stand in the middle, which is on none of them, and read the order as it fills. I marked the middle safe and underlined it, twice.",
      threadweaver: "He sewed his landings across the crater, mark to mark, every line laid before the first stitch. The middle lay inside none of them. We held the middle and let him pull the thread tight around us.",
      tidecaller: "He skims the whole crater and comes down on each mark in turn, all drawn at the start. The middle is still water: on none of them. We waited there and let the waves go round.",
      lanternkeeper: "He skimmed the crater and came down on each mark in turn, and all of it was drawn at the start. The middle was on none of it, so we gathered there and I set the lanterns in a ring around us.",
      kenseier: "A landing on every mark. He gave the whole run before the first stroke, and the middle was on none of it. I stood in the middle and answered each landing in turn. Each had the same count.",
      houndmaster: "A landing on every mark, all drawn first. Middle of the crater is on none of them. Held the middle, dogs close. Watched the order fill. He came round, never in.",
    } },
    'Wingbeat': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "His wings beat as he flew, and everything near the line was shoved away from it. I stood far enough from the line that it only nudged me, and held my footing. A man can be moved. The trick is to be asked politely.",
      gloomsworn: "The wingbeat threw everything near his flight line outward. I stood well back from it and felt only a nudge. Nearer, you meet the wall; I keep a good distance from both.",
      thresher: "His wings stirred a gale down the dive road, and anyone near it was flung like ash from a bonfire. I stood well back from the road, not just off it, and kept clear of the walls besides. A fine wind, if you are not in it.",
      bloodreaver: "A shove from his wings, and everyone near the line goes flying! I like a good shove. Stay well back from the line, or be carried away from it, or into the wall.",
      runeguard: "The wings lift as he enters the line. Those near it are thrown outward, so stand well back from it, or be ready for the wall. A footnote for the wall, in red.",
      gorgemaw: "The wings carried a gust that threw the diners from the table's middle. I took my plate to the far end, well away from the line, and stayed seated. Blown, not eaten.",
      oathbroker: "The Wingbeat is a rider to the dive: all parties near the line are removed from it by force. The question is where they are removed to. Stand well back, or choose the landing.",
      ironpalm: "His wings spoke, and everything near the line was pushed away. I stood far from it, and was not pushed.",
      ranger: "Wingbeat. Everyone near the line is thrown clear of it. Stood off, well back, no push. Simple, if you like walls.",
      shadowblade: "His wings threw everyone near the line aside, which was rude of him. I was already far from it, naturally. Neither he nor the wall saw me.",
      pyromancer: "He flapped, and the gust flung my friends across the floor! Stay well back from the line, or be thrown into the wall. A dramatic wind machine.",
      thermist: "Wingbeat: cold air, everyone near the line thrown out. Stay far from it, not just clear of it. I stood close once, and swung, and hit the wall. Wall: hard. Noted.",
      driftblade: "His wings threw a gust across the line and I rode it, out along the push and through the gap at the end, round and away, never landing, and not into the wall.",
      marionettist: "The star's wings fanned the stage and threw the cast to the wings. Stand well back from his entrance, or be carried off. (My Doll was blown into the orchestra. Unhurt.)",
      cleric: "His wings threw us away from the line, and one of us met the wall and cried out. Stand well back, not merely clear, I tell them now, and I mended what I could.",
      grovekeeper: "The wings stirred a gale along the line and everyone near it was blown away like leaves, dear. Stand well back, and the gale only ruffles. Keep from the edge, too.",
      chronomancer: "The gust comes just before the dive itself, and everyone near the line is thrown. I stood far back at that moment. Last time, which was next time, I stood closer, and learned.",
      alembist: "The Wingbeat, a gust along the line that throws everyone near it. Stand well back, or accept the wall. The wall is not a cure. I have the bruise to prove it.",
      threadweaver: "His wings pulled every thread near the line out sideways, and one stitch hit the wall. Stand well back from the line, and keep clear of the edge.",
      tidecaller: "On the hard nights his wings shoved everyone near the flight line outward, toward the wall. Well back is not the same as merely clear: we stood far off from the line and felt only a nudge. Big water pushes everything near it.",
      lanternkeeper: "The hard nights added a wingbeat that shoved anyone near his flight line out toward the wall. Merely clear was not enough; far was. We stood far, felt a nudge, and I kept my lanterns back there with us.",
      kenseier: "His wings beat as he flew, and everything near the line was shoved away from it. I stood far enough from it that it only nudged me. A push is not a wound. Let it carry you, then plant your feet again.",
      houndmaster: "Wings beat and everything near the line was thrown clear. Stood well back so it only nudged me, dogs behind. A push isn't a wound. Plant your feet again.",
    } },
    'Cinder Rain': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Embers fell across the crater, each in a little circle that burned afterwards. I held my ground, watched for the circles, and moved only when one landed near me. A man under rain does not need to run.",
      gloomsworn: "Embers fell everywhere in small circles, each leaving a burning patch. I walked clear of whichever came near me and no further. There was no hurry in it.",
      thresher: "Embers dropped in small circles all over the crater, each leaving a burning pool. I kept moving, watched where the next would rise, and went round. Plenty of fresh pits out there; I took none, and the dancing was good.",
      bloodreaver: "Sparks! All over the floor, and each leaves a pool. A scar for every one, if you like, and I do not, today. Keep moving, and watch the little circles.",
      runeguard: "Small circles bloom one after another across the whole crater. Each leaves a pool, so watch where the next one appears, and do not stand still. I read the pattern on the third; the first two were tuition.",
      gorgemaw: "A shower of sparks from the great oven. Little circles of heat, each leaving a puddle of sauce. I ate none, and moved along the table. Over-seasoned.",
      oathbroker: "The Rain is a series of small claims, each against a named tile, each leaving a lien on the ground. They come one after another. Clear each in turn.",
      ironpalm: "Embers fell, one at a time, all over. Each left a small fire. I stepped aside from each in turn.",
      ranger: "Cinder rain. Small circles, one after another, all over the floor. Each leaves a pool. Kept moving, kept clear.",
      shadowblade: "Sparks fell all over the crater, and each left a pool. A fine thing to watch, from the shadows. I kept moving, between the circles.",
      pyromancer: "It RAINED fire! Little circles blooming all over the floor, each one leaving a puddle of lava! I have never been so happy or so busy. Encore, please.",
      thermist: "Embers, hot, small, many. Step aside from each and off the pools. I ran hot with joy and stepped in one. Carefully, now.",
      driftblade: "The embers fell all over the crater and I wove between them, through the gaps, round the pools, never stopping, and the floor behind me burned in my footsteps.",
      marionettist: "A shower of sparks from the flies, each landing in a small bright circle, and each leaving a hot patch on the boards. The cast kept to the dark. (The Doll adored it.)",
      cleric: "Embers fell in little circles all over the floor, each leaving a pool. Nobody stood still, which for once I had no need to preach.",
      grovekeeper: "The embers came like seeds scattered by a careless hand, and where each fell, a pool of fire. Keep moving, step round them, and plant nothing there. My lot did.",
      chronomancer: "Each ember lands after the one before. I stepped aside from the first, and the next, and the next. I kept the rhythm, mostly.",
      alembist: "Many small circles, each leaving a pool: keep moving, step clear of each in turn. Do not shelve anything on the floor. This is a rule now.",
      threadweaver: "Embers fell like pins across the whole floor, each a small hole in the cloth. Step clear of each, and off the pools they leave.",
      tidecaller: "Embers fell all over the crater, each leaving a small pool that burned. I kept walking, stepped round each as it landed, kept off the pools afterwards, and fed the water in little drops. Rain is patient, and so are we.",
      lanternkeeper: "Embers fell all over the crater, each leaving a small pool that burned. I kept walking, stepped round each one as it landed, and kept my lanterns off the pools. Nobody stood still under that rain.",
      kenseier: "Each ember had its small circle drawn before it fell, and burned the ground after. I stood quiet and stepped aside only for the ones that came near. Rain asks no one to run, only to be elsewhere.",
      houndmaster: "Each ember had its small circle drawn before it fell. Kept moving, stepped out of those that came near, and kept the dogs moving too. Pools stay a while. Mind them.",
    } },
    'Heart Flare': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "The Heart flared. One of us was marked for a hit we had to share, so I closed the line on them; and the moment it landed we scattered, each to our own patch. Two orders in one breath. The line took both.",
      gloomsworn: "A ring over one of us to be shared, so we closed in on it. The instant it landed there were rings over everyone, and we went apart, each to a space of our own. First gather, then leave.",
      thresher: "First a ring over one of us to share, so we all stood in close, snug as one cloak among many. The moment it fell, a ring over each head, so out to our own ground. Together, then apart, one step after the other. Off we go!",
      bloodreaver: "A flare! First everyone piles onto the one who is marked, then we all fly apart before the second blow. Barely a scratch, and a good dance.",
      runeguard: "A ring over one of us, to be shared; and the instant it lands, marks over all. Together first, then apart. The order is the whole text, and I underlined it twice.",
      gorgemaw: "A course for the table, then dessert for each. First all sit down together at the marked seat and share it, then each rises and takes a plate of their own.",
      oathbroker: "The Flare is a two-part instrument: a shared debt, then individual claims. The order is everything. Stand together, then stand alone.",
      ironpalm: "Once to all of us in the ring. Then to each of us alone. We stood together first, then apart.",
      ranger: "Ring on one of us, share it. Then marks on everyone. Together, then apart.",
      shadowblade: "The mark flared, and I stood in the ring with the rest, which I hate, and then I went to my own corner as fast as I could, which I love.",
      pyromancer: "A shared flare, and then marks over everyone's head! Together first, then apart, as fast as possible. I forgot the second part once. Educational, and loud.",
      thermist: "Ring: shared, hot. Everyone in. Then marks: out, apart, each alone. I swung between the two and ran hot, then cold. Out. Apart. Then out some more.",
      driftblade: "The ring came and I hated standing in it with everyone, then the marks came and I swept out of the ring and across the floor and through the others' patches, to my own.",
      marionettist: "Curtain call in two parts: the whole troupe bows together, and the moment it ends, each steps downstage alone. (The Doll was always alone. It's a gift.)",
      cleric: "He flared. First we stood together in the ring, and the hit was shared; then marks fell on each of us, and we scattered. I mended the ones who forgot the second half.",
      grovekeeper: "First the weather gathers us in, and the flare breaks over the lot; then it breaks again over each alone. Together, then scatter. Know which half is next.",
      chronomancer: "The second part lands just after the first, and I moved on that note. The first is shared, the second is not. I stood in the ring a beat too long once.",
      alembist: "A shared dose, then a separate one for each: together first, then apart. Keep the flasks in two groups. Group one: shared. Group two: personal. No mixing! Ever.",
      threadweaver: "The first was shared, so I drew every thread in tight; the second was for each, so I cut them loose the instant the first landed. A neat piece of cutting.",
      tidecaller: "A ring over one of us to be shared, and we closed on it together. The moment it landed, marks fell over every head and we scattered, each to our own ground. In, then out, like the tide, and a good singer knows which half she is in.",
      lanternkeeper: "A ring over one of us, to be shared, and we closed in on it together. The moment it landed, marks fell over every head and we scattered, each to our own ground. In first, then out. I left a lantern at the ring and walked away from it.",
      kenseier: "The Heart flared. One of the others was marked for a blow that wanted sharing, so we closed on them, and once it landed we parted, each to a patch of our own. Together, then apart: two beats.",
      houndmaster: "Heart flared. One of the others was marked to share, so we closed on them. Then marks on everyone, and each went to their own patch. Together, then apart. Dogs the same.",
    } },
    'Take Flight': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He spread his wings and a gust went through every one of us, and then he was gone from the ground. No shield for a gust. I kept the line close to the healer, stood our ground, and watched the sky for where he would come down.",
      gloomsworn: "His wings opened and the gust went through the whole crater, and then there was no one to hold. I let it pass. A watch with nothing to watch is the dull part of the job. I looked up, as I have before.",
      thresher: "Wings out, a gust over every one of us, and off the ground he went for good. Light work for the healer, dear, and nothing to step aside from. I stayed mended and kept an eye on the sky, where the next dance would come from.",
      bloodreaver: "He flapped and the whole crater got a face full of wind, and up he went. A small bruise, nothing worth the name. I wanted him back down at once. Waiting is the worst part.",
      runeguard: "The wings spread and a gust passed over all of us at once: a small toll, paid by everyone alike, with no ward to turn it. Then he left the ground, and I began watching the air for his return.",
      gorgemaw: "The host spread his wings, blew a hot gust across every seat, and left the table. No dish worth mentioning. I stayed topped up and waited to see what he would serve from the air.",
      oathbroker: "A notice, served by wind upon every party at once: his wings spread, a gust crossed the floor, and he removed himself from the jurisdiction. The sum owed was small. We remained mended and waited for his return.",
      ironpalm: "His wings opened. The wind reached every one of us, light, and he left the ground. We stood still and let it pass. Then we watched the sky.",
      ranger: "Wings out, gust over everyone. Light. Then it left the ground and stayed gone. Held position, kept my eyes on the sky, and waited to see where it dropped.",
      shadowblade: "The mark spread its wings and blew a gust through the lot of us before leaving the ground. Nothing to hide from. I stayed topped up and in the shadows, and watched the sky for its return.",
      pyromancer: "He spread those great wings and blew a gale across all of us, and then he was gone, straight up! A small thump of a hit, hardly worth mentioning. I looked skyward and waited for the next dazzling entrance.",
      thermist: "Wings out: a gust, hot, on all of us. Small. Then up, off the ground. Be mended and wait. I turned my face to the cold sky and watched for where he would land.",
      driftblade: "He threw his wings open and the gust ran through us all, and then he was off the ground and gone, which I envied, and I kept moving below, eyes on the sky, ready to cut aside the moment he turned toward the floor.",
      marionettist: "Our star spread his wings, a gust swept the whole house, and up he went into the flies. A small toll on the company, mended at once. (The Doll watched the ceiling, and so did I.)",
      cleric: "His wings opened and a gust went over every one of us, small but sudden. I mended the scuffs at once, and then we all looked up, for he had left the ground and would come down somewhere.",
      grovekeeper: "He spread his wings and a hot gust crossed the whole crater, like a wind before rain. Small, and over quickly. I mended what it scuffed and watched the sky, since he has left the ground and will drop again.",
      chronomancer: "The wings open, and the gust lands on everyone just after. Small. He leaves the ground on the beat that follows. I mended the scuffs and watched the sky, the way I always have.",
      alembist: "A small gust across the whole party as he left the ground. A little something for everyone, then eyes up for his return. Shelf check, while the sky is busy.",
      threadweaver: "The wings spread and a gust tugged at every thread, small and quick. Then he was off the ground, out of reach. I tied up the slack, mended the scuffs, and kept a sharp eye on the sky.",
      tidecaller: "His wings opened and a small gust ran over every one of us, like a ripple on a pond. Then he left the ground, and I held the water ready and watched the sky for the next swell.",
      lanternkeeper: "He opened his wings and a gust passed over all of us, quick and small. Then he left the ground and the crater was dark where he had been. I trimmed the wicks and watched the sky for his return.",
      kenseier: "His wings opened, a gust went over us all, and he left the ground. No stroke to meet. We were mended in the gap, and then we watched the sky, because the next blow comes from there.",
      houndmaster: "Wings out, gust over the lot of us, and off the ground he went. Small knock. Dogs kept low, healer topped us up, eyes on the sky for where he would come down.",
    } },
    'Skyward': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He climbed out of the crater in a wide circle round the rim, and a gust reached every one of us as he went. Nothing to block and nothing to run from. I kept the line close to the healer and did not chase him.",
      gloomsworn: "He went up in a great ring round the rim, and the wind of it touched all of us lightly. No one needed to dodge anything. I looked at the sky a moment, then at the floor. He was out of reach, so I did not reach.",
      thresher: "Up he went in one wide circle, and a light gust fell on the whole party as he climbed. Nothing to step aside from, dear, so I let the healer top us up and did not chase him. No use dancing with a partner who has left the floor.",
      bloodreaver: "He launched himself round the whole rim and a gust slapped every one of us. A gust, no more. Nothing to dodge, nothing to hit, he is out of reach, so I stood waiting for him to come back. Tease.",
      runeguard: "He rises in a single great circle about the rim, and a light gust marks his ascent for all of us at once. There is nothing to read and nothing to avoid; he cannot be struck, so do not try. Be mended, and wait for him to descend.",
      gorgemaw: "The beast left the table in one wide lap round the rim, and a light gust passed over every seat. A palate cleanser, nothing more. No dodging and no chasing; he was out of reach. I stayed topped up and waited for the next course.",
      oathbroker: "A short recess, announced by a gust that reached every one of us, lightly: his ascent, in one wide circle round the rim. Nothing is demanded of the party but to remain mended. He cannot be struck while aloft, and I filed no claim.",
      ironpalm: "He rose in a circle, and a light wind reached each of us. Nothing to answer. I stood where I was and did not chase him. He was gone from reach, and the wind passed.",
      ranger: "Wide circle round the rim as it climbed. Gust on everyone, light. No line on it once it left the ground, so I stopped drawing and let the healer top us up.",
      shadowblade: "The mark made a show of leaving, a wide circle round the rim, and a light gust brushed us all. No dodging, nothing to hide from. It was out of reach, so I simply waited, unseen, for the next entrance.",
      pyromancer: "He soared round the crater in one huge ring, and the wind of it touched everyone! Nothing to dodge, nothing to burn, since he is out of reach. I let our healer top me up and admired the view. Show-off.",
      thermist: "Cold sky, hot wind. He climbed out in a ring round the rim, and a light gust reached all of us. Nothing to sidestep. Be mended and wait, because he can't be hit up there. I stopped swinging and let myself settle.",
      driftblade: "He went up in one great sweeping ring round the rim and the gust came after, lightly, and I wanted so badly to follow him through the sky and could not, so I ran a lap of the floor in his wake and came back whole.",
      marionettist: "The star rose into the flies in one wide circle, and a light gust swept the house. No cue for the company but to stand mended. He cannot be struck from up there. (The Doll bowed to the empty stage.)",
      cleric: "He circled the rim as he rose, and a light gust fell on all of us. Nothing to dodge, only to be ready. I mended the ones it had scuffed before the next thing came, and told the youngsters not to chase him up into the sky.",
      grovekeeper: "He climbed out in a wide ring round the rim, and the gust came down on the whole lot of us like a light squall. Nothing to hide from. Let it pass, mend what it scuffs, and do not try to reach what has left the field.",
      chronomancer: "He rises in one wide circle and the gust lands on everyone a moment after he leaves the ground. Light, and nothing to step from. He cannot be hit up there, so I mended the scuffs in that quiet beat, as I always do.",
      alembist: "A light gust across the whole party as he climbs, circling the rim. A small dose each, and nothing to dodge. He is out of reach, so no use wasting effort chasing him. Label: patience.",
      threadweaver: "He went up in one wide circle round the rim, and a light gust tugged at every one of us. No need to cut a thread; there was nothing to dodge. I tied off the slack, mended the scuffs, and left the sky to him.",
      tidecaller: "He rose in a great slow circle round the rim, and a light gust rolled over every one of us. A swell too small to run from. He was out of reach, so I kept the water slack and waited for him to come back down.",
      lanternkeeper: "He climbed out in one wide circle round the rim, and a light gust reached all of us. Nothing to dodge, nothing to hit. I trimmed the wicks, kept my lanterns where we stood, and did not follow him into the dark.",
      kenseier: "He rose in a wide circle round the rim, and a gust reached every one of us, lightly. He was out of reach and the gust had no edge, so we stood and were mended. No stroke to meet until he came down.",
      houndmaster: "Went up in a wide ring round the rim, gust over all of us. Light. No cover to run to. Dogs in close, healer near, and no chasing. You can't track what's gone up.",
    } },
    'The Tyrant Wakes': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "He reared on the open floor and roared, and it reached every one of us. The rim began to glow and stayed lit. Nothing to block, so I closed the line up by the healer and took it standing. A glow is not a wound.",
      gloomsworn: "He stood up on the ground and roared the whole crater awake. It went through all of us. The rim took on a glow and kept it, like a lamp left for someone. I stood by the healer and let it pass.",
      thresher: "The old tyrant rose on his hind legs and roared, and it fell on every one of us at once. Nothing to step from, dear. I came to it mended. Afterwards the rim began to glow, slow as a coal catching, and it never dimmed.",
      bloodreaver: "He reared up like he owned the place, and roared right through us all! The rim lit up behind him and stayed lit. No scar in it, just noise. I stood by the healer and grinned through the whole thing.",
      runeguard: "He rears and the whole crater is addressed at once; no ward turns it, so each of us arrives mended. In the margin: the rim begins to glow as he finishes, and does not dim. A signature, I think, and nothing more, yet.",
      gorgemaw: "The host stood and addressed every guest, at volume, and it reached every seat. The rim began to glow like a grill coming up to heat, and stayed so. I took the roar on a full plate and ate through it.",
      oathbroker: "A proclamation, read to every party at once, and no clause excuses any of us. The rim of the crater began to glow as he finished, as though a seal were pressed in wax. We stood mended beforehand. Nothing to contest, as yet.",
      ironpalm: "He rose and spoke to the whole crater. We were already mended and stood still. The rim began to glow after the last word and did not stop. I let the sound pass through me and said nothing back.",
      ranger: "Rears on the ground, roars. Reaches everyone. No cover anywhere. Mended first, rode it out. The rim glowed after and stayed lit. Landmark, nothing more.",
      shadowblade: "The mark stood up on the ground, in plain view, and roared at everyone, me included. Terribly impolite. I made sure I was whole beforehand. The rim began to glow when it finished, and I took note: nobody hides on a lit edge.",
      pyromancer: "He stood up and roared at all of us, and the rim of the crater lit up behind him like a stage! I was mended before the roar and cast straight through it. A glow that stays lit! I must learn that.",
      thermist: "Roar: wide, low, everyone. Be mended before. Then the rim began to glow and kept glowing, red at the edge, and I ran hot just looking. Read it as colour, not damage. Back to work, steady.",
      driftblade: "He reared on the ground and roared, and there was no way through or round it, just the sound through all of us, and I took it whole and kept moving, and the rim lit up and stayed lit, bright as a distant fire.",
      marionettist: "The star rose to his full height, centre stage, and roared at the whole house. The company came to it mended and took it as one. As he finished, the footlights along the rim came up and stayed up. (The Doll curtsied.)",
      cleric: "He stood up and roared over the whole crater, and it fell on every one of us. I mended everyone before it, not after, and then a little more. The rim began to glow when he finished, and stayed lit. A sign, perhaps, that he is not done.",
      grovekeeper: "He rose and roared, and it came over us like a wind before a storm, every one of my lot at once. I had the mending in them before it broke. Afterwards the rim began to glow and kept on. Weather like that takes a whole season.",
      chronomancer: "He rears, and the roar lands as his jaws close, on everyone. I had us mended just before, and again after. Then the rim began to glow, and I cannot remember it ever not glowing. Which was last time, I suppose.",
      alembist: "A roar across the whole party, from a dragon standing up on the ground. Everyone topped up beforehand, nothing to dodge. Side effect: the rim of the crater begins to glow and does not stop. Noted, and kept the corks tight.",
      threadweaver: "He reared and roared, and it reached every stitch together, with nothing to tie off. We were mended beforehand and took it close together. Then the rim began to glow along its whole edge, like a hot seam, and stayed that way.",
      tidecaller: "He stood up and roared across the crater like a breaking wave, and it reached all of us at once. I raised the water before it came. When the roar ebbed, the rim began to glow and kept its glow. A long evening still ahead.",
      lanternkeeper: "He reared up on the ground and roared, and it reached all of us. I lit every one of us before it came, so it fell on a mended party. Afterward the rim began to glow all by itself, and it stayed lit. A light I did not set.",
      kenseier: "He rose on the bare floor and roared the length of the crater. No beat to count and nothing to meet, so we stood by the healer, mended first. The rim began to glow after, and stayed lit.",
      houndmaster: "Reared on the open floor and roared. Reached every one of us, dogs flat to the ground. Stayed by the healer, mended first. The rim started glowing when he finished and kept it.",
    } },
    'Marked Prey': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "He no longer picked a pit. He picked one of us: a sign over the head, or a number for each in order. The ring follows its carrier until it settles. The carrier takes it to the edge, away from the line, and runs out. The rest of us keep clear.",
      gloomsworn: "A person this time, not a place. Someone wore a sign, or each of us wore a number in turn, and a ring dogged them. They took it to the far edge, where molten stone does least harm, and stepped out before it dropped. I stayed out of everyone's ring.",
      thresher: "Now he names a dancer instead of a floor. A sign over one head, or a number over each in turn, and a great ring trails after them. Lead it to the rim, dear, and step clear before it sets. The rest of us stay away from the ring, and from the pool he leaves.",
      bloodreaver: "He picked ME, or whoever has the number! A ring that follows its target and then drops. The wearer drags it out to the edge, away from everyone, and jumps clear before it lands. Everyone else, keep out. I like being chosen. I like being clear of it more.",
      runeguard: "The omen has moved from the pit to the person: a sign over one head, or numerals in the order of his landings. The ring trails its bearer and is then fixed. So: bear it to the rim, leave it before it sets, and let no one else stand within it.",
      gorgemaw: "My host stopped choosing a plate and chose a guest: a sign over one head, or a number in the order of service. The ring trails them like a waiter. The guest carries it to the edge of the room and steps out before it is set down. Nobody else sits near it.",
      oathbroker: "The landing is now served on a named person, not a named spot: a sign over one head, or numbers in order of service. The ring follows the party served. They lead it to the rim, away from the rest of us, and leave it before it settles. No co-signatory stands in another's ring.",
      ironpalm: "He addressed a single hero this time. A sign hung over one head, or numbers came in sequence. The ring went where they went. Walk it away, step aside, leave the hot stone behind. The rest of us gave every ring room.",
      ranger: "Mark on a hero, not the ground. Sign over one head, or numbers in order. The ring tracks its carrier, then sets. Haul it to the rim, step out before it lands, leave the molten pool where nobody needs to stand.",
      shadowblade: "The mark chose a face instead of a floor: a sign over one head, or a number for each in turn. The ring follows its carrier and sets. They lead it to the rim and slip out before it lands. I kept well away from everyone's ring, mine included.",
      pyromancer: "He picks an audience member now, and a ring chases them around! A sign over one head, or a number over each in turn. The chosen one drags it to the edge and runs out before it falls. The rest of us stand well clear. Molten stone stays hot. I checked, regrettably.",
      thermist: "Marked: a sign over one head, or numbers over each in order. A ring trails its carrier. Hot mistake: dragging it through the party. Cold plan: carry it to the rim, step out before it sets, and leave the pool out there. Others stay far off.",
      driftblade: "He chose runners instead of ground: a sign over one head, or a number over each of us in turn, and a ring that follows. I took mine out toward the rim, round and through the empty floor and out of it before it fell, and the others kept clear of every ring.",
      marionettist: "The star now chooses his mark from the company: a sign over one head, or a number each, in order of entrance. The ring follows its carrier like a spotlight. Lead it to the edge of the stage and step out before it falls. The others stay in the dark.",
      cleric: "He chose one of my young ones, with a sign over the head, or each in turn with a number, and a great ring followed them. I told the chosen to run it to the rim and step out before it fell, and the rest of us to keep well away from it.",
      grovekeeper: "The storm picked one of my lot, not a spot: a sign over one head, or a number each in turn, and a ring that trails them like a shadow. They walked it out to the rim and left it there. The rest of us kept off the ring and off the burnt ground.",
      chronomancer: "A number over each of us in turn, or a sign over one, and the ring follows its bearer and sets on a beat. The bearer leads it to the rim and steps out before it sets. I mended on the off-beat. Everyone kept clear of the others' rings, this time.",
      alembist: "A landing aimed at a person, a sign over one head or numbers in order. The ring tracks its carrier. Carry it to the rim, step out before it sets, and nobody else near. The pool it leaves stays hot. Label the rim: spill here.",
      threadweaver: "He aims at one of my stitches, not a patch of floor: a sign over one head, or numbers in the order he comes. The thread follows them, and the ring with it. Carry it out to the rim, step out before it sets, and keep every other stitch well off.",
      tidecaller: "He chose a swimmer, not a shore: a sign over one head, or a number over each in turn, and the ring trailed after them like a rip. The chosen led it out to the rim and stepped from it before it came down. The rest of us kept well clear of it.",
      lanternkeeper: "He aimed at a person, not a place: a sign over one head, or a number over each in turn. The ring followed them. They carried it to the rim, as I would carry a lamp to the far wall, and stepped out before it set. We kept our own lights well clear.",
      kenseier: "The target was a person, never a patch of floor. The sign or number marked who, and the number gave the order of landings. A bearer led the ring to the rim and left it a moment before it fell. We kept well out of everyone's.",
      houndmaster: "Went after one of us this time, not a pit. Sign over a head, or numbers in order. Whoever wears it hauls the ring out to the rim and steps off before it drops. Dogs kept well off every ring. Pools stay hot, so leave them at the edge.",
    } },
    'Ember Devils': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Rings dogged every one of us, and each left a small whirl of fire where it ended. Touch a whirl and it bursts over the ones beside it. The line stood wide apart before it set, and afterwards each of us stepped off our own and went round the rest.",
      gloomsworn: "Each of us got a ring that followed us, and when it ended a small whirl of fire stood where we were. Touch one and it bursts over everyone close. We went well apart first, then walked out of our own and around the rest. I walked softly. I have practice.",
      thresher: "A ring on every one of us, and then a little whirl of fire where each had stood. Touch one and it bursts over whoever is near it. So we spread out first, each on our own ground, then stepped away from our own whirl, round the others'. A careful dance, and a short one.",
      bloodreaver: "Whirls of fire under every one of us! Touch one, and it goes off in the face of everyone close. So I stood well apart from my friends as the rings set, and walked out of mine without stepping on theirs. No glory in hurting a friend by accident.",
      runeguard: "A ring followed each of us, and where it ended a whirl of fire was left. A whirl that is touched bursts upon all who stand near it. The order: go far apart while the rings are following; then leave your own whirl and pass wide of the others'.",
      gorgemaw: "A ring followed every guest, and where it ended a small cinder-whirl was left on every plate. Touch one and it bursts over everyone near it. So we sat far apart while the rings set, then stood from our own plates and walked round the others'. Seasoned, and not by me.",
      oathbroker: "A ring followed each party, and where it ended a whirl of fire was lodged under him. Contact with any whirl sets it off against all within reach. Hence the instruction: stand well apart as the rings settle, then leave your own whirl and keep clear of every other.",
      ironpalm: "A ring followed each of us, and a whirl was left where it ended. A touched whirl bursts on all near it. We stood far apart, each alone. Then each of us walked away from our own and round every other. Do not touch what is not yours.",
      ranger: "Ring follows each of us, whirl of fire left under each. Touch one and it bursts on whoever is near. Spaced out wide before the rings set, then walked clear of my own and round everyone else's. No crowding.",
      shadowblade: "Everyone got a ring that followed them, and then a small whirl of fire underfoot. Touch one and it bursts over everyone near it, which is no way to treat a quiet evening. I kept well apart, then slipped out of mine and round the rest.",
      pyromancer: "A ring on each of us, and then a tiny whirl of fire where each stood! Touch one and it goes off on everyone nearby. So we spread far apart as the rings set, I stepped out of my own, and then went round the others' with great care. My own fire, mostly respected.",
      thermist: "Rings on everyone, whirls of fire after. Touch one and it bursts on whoever stands near. So: far apart while the rings follow, out of my own whirl, round every other. Hot mistake: huddling for comfort. Don't.",
      driftblade: "A ring on each of us, following, and then a whirl of fire where each stood, and I hate being held in place, so I ran wide while the rings set, then slid out of mine and wove through the gaps between the others' without brushing one.",
      marionettist: "Each of the company wore a ring that followed them, and where it ended a small whirl of fire was left on the boards. A touched whirl bursts on everyone near it. So the company fanned wide, each on their own mark, then stepped off it and round the others'.",
      cleric: "A ring followed every one of us, and where it ended a small whirl of fire was left behind. A touched whirl bursts on everyone near it. I asked them to stand well apart first, then to leave their own whirl and go round the others'. Gently, children, gently.",
      grovekeeper: "Every one of my lot got a ring that followed them, and then a whirl of fire where each stood, like a dust devil in a dry field. Touch one and it bursts over those near it. We spread wide first, then stepped off our own and went round the rest.",
      chronomancer: "The rings follow each of us, and just after they end, a small whirl of fire stands under each. Touch one and it bursts on all near it. We were far apart before the end. Then each stepped out of their own and round the others'. The tidy way.",
      alembist: "Each of us took a ring, and where we stood it left a small whirl of fire. Touch one and it bursts on everyone near. So stand well apart before the rings set, leave your own whirl behind, and keep clear of the others'. Keep the flasks apart too.",
      threadweaver: "Every stitch of mine got a ring that followed it, and a whirl of fire left where each stood. Touch one and it bursts on whoever is near. I cut the threads wide while the rings set, then walked out of my own whirl and round every other. No tangles.",
      tidecaller: "Rings followed us, and then small spouts of fire rose where each of us had stood, like waterspouts on a flat sea. Touch one and it bursts on whoever is near. We drifted far apart while the rings set, then each waded clear of our own and around the others'.",
      lanternkeeper: "Every one of us was followed by a ring, and where it ended a small whirl of fire stood. Touching one sets it off on all near it. I kept my friends and my lanterns well apart until the rings had set, then walked out of mine and round the rest, one at a time.",
      kenseier: "A ring followed each of us, then a small whirl of fire was left where it ended. A touched whirl bursts on all within reach. We spaced out wide before it set, then each stepped out of their own and passed wide of the rest. A whirl wants only a touch.",
      houndmaster: "Ring followed each of us, whirl of fire left under every one. Touch a whirl and it bursts on all near it. Spread wide before the rings set, dogs too, then stepped off mine and round the rest. Don't let the lads sniff at them.",
    } },
    'Molten Trail': { hard: false, extreme: true, learned: {
      bastion: "One of the others got a sign over the head that meant keep moving, and a ring fell where they stood, again and again, a short breath apart. They walked the rim, away from us, and never stopped. The rest of us stayed in my ring and took the bites without them.",
      gloomsworn: "A sign sent one of us walking alone, with rings of fire landing behind each step. They stayed on the move along the rim and never looked back. The rest of us took the bites without them. I know that kind of solitude.",
      thresher: "One dancer was called away by a sign over the head, and a ring fell at their heels, then another, then another. Keep walking, dear, along the rim, away from the party, and never stand still. The rest of us stayed in the big ring and covered for them.",
      bloodreaver: "One of us got a walking sign, and rings of fire dropped on their heels, over and over! They kept running the rim, away from everyone, and the burning ground they left behind was well out of our way. I stayed in the big ring with the rest and bled for it.",
      runeguard: "A sign of walking over one head, and a ring falls at that hero's heels, again and again. So: keep walking along the rim, away from the party, and never stop. The hunted one is excused from the shared ring; the rest of us carry their share.",
      gorgemaw: "One guest was sent off the table with a walking sign, and a hot dish fell at their heels, then another, and another. They kept moving along the rim, away from the rest of us, and never sat. The rest of us took the big plate between us, one short.",
      oathbroker: "One co-signatory was released from the shared ring and made liable for another duty: a walking sign, and rings falling at his heels one after another. He is to keep moving along the rim, away from the party. The rest of us bear the shared sum without him.",
      ironpalm: "One was sent walking. A sign said so, and rings fell at their heels. They kept to the rim, away from us, and did not stop. The rest of us took the bites without them.",
      ranger: "One of us got a walking sign. Rings drop on their heels, one after another. Keep moving, along the rim, away from the rest. The pools stay behind, out of our way. The rest of us shared the big ring, one short.",
      shadowblade: "One of the others was sent walking, a sign over the head, and rings of fire dropped on their heels. They walked the rim, away from everyone, and never stopped, which is a very visible way to spend an evening. I kept to the ring with the rest.",
      pyromancer: "Someone got a walking sign and fire fell at their heels, ring after ring! A tremendous spectacle, if you are not the one doing the walking. Keep going along the rim, away from the party. The rest of us stood in our knight's ring, one short.",
      thermist: "Marked to move: one hero, rings landing wherever they were a moment ago. Keep walking the rim, never stand, never cut back toward the party. Pools stay behind them. The rest of us stood in the big ring, short a body.",
      driftblade: "Someone got a sign to keep moving and rings fell at their heels, over and over, so they ran the rim away from us and never once stood, and I envied them, truly, while the rest of us stood in the knight's ring and took the bites.",
      marionettist: "One of the company was sent into the wings on a long walk, a sign over the head, and a ring fell at their heels, then again. Keep moving along the rim, away from the stage. The rest of us stood in the leading man's ring, one short.",
      cleric: "One of my young ones was sent walking, a sign over their head and a ring of fire falling at their heels, again and again. I told them to keep moving along the rim, away from us, and not to look back. The rest of us shared the big ring, and I mended the gaps.",
      grovekeeper: "One of my lot was told to walk by a sign over the head, and the ember rings fell wherever they had just been. Keep moving, keep to the rim, and the burnt ground trails behind and away from us. The rest stood in the knight's ring and endured the bites.",
      chronomancer: "One hero wears the walking sign, and rings fall at their heels, each a beat after the last. They kept moving along the rim, away from us, and never stood. The rest of us shared the big ring, one short. I had expected that, I think.",
      alembist: "One patient hunted by rings of fire, a walking sign over the head. Prescription: keep moving along the rim, away from the others, and never stop. The rest of us stay in the big ring, one short. Label the walker: do not stop.",
      threadweaver: "One of my stitches was sent off alone with a walking sign, and rings fell at their heels, one after another. I cut that thread loose for the time being. They kept moving along the rim, away from the hem, and the rest of us held together in the ring.",
      tidecaller: "One of us was sent walking, a sign over the head, and rings of fire fell at their heels like drops from a leaking roof. They walked the rim, away from us, and never stood. The rest of us shared the big ring and I rationed the water a little thinner.",
      lanternkeeper: "A sign over one head said move. That one walked the rim, away from the rest of us, and a ring fell at every step. I hung nothing on them; light would only have slowed them. We others kept together in the knight's ring, one short.",
      kenseier: "One of the others walked a different road, a sign over the head, and rings fell at their heels each on its own beat. Keep moving; a standing target is a struck target. The rest of us kept to the knight's ring and bore the bites, one short.",
      houndmaster: "One of us sent walking, sign on their head, rings landing on their heels. Rim, away from the rest, no stopping, dogs and all kept clear of that trail. We others stood in the shield-man's ring, a body short.",
    } },
  },
};
